{"id":"civicplus-5-01182023-44","platform":"civicplus","body":"Planning Commission","name":"PLANNING COMMISSION MEETING 1-18-2023 (PDF)","date":"2023-01-18","time":"","canceled":false,"agenda_url":"https://www.cityofavalon.com/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_01182023-44","minutes_url":"https://www.cityofavalon.com/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Minutes/_01182023-44","packet_url":"","category_id":5,"agenda_text":"CITY OF AVALON PLANNING COMMISSION AGENDA\n  City Hall Council Chambers ∙ 410 Avalon Canyon Road, Avalon, CA\n  Regular Meeting – Wednesday January 18, 2023 at 5:00 p.m.\n\nThis meeting is viewable in person at the Council Chambers or via livestream:\n\n  Facebook: (http://www.facebook.com/CityOfAvalonOfficial)\n  YouTube (http://www.youtube.com/cityofavalonca)\n  Website (http://www.cityofavalon.com/meetings)\n  Cable (Catalina Broadband Solutions Channel 3)\n\nThe Council Chambers can accommodate a maximum of 30 participants, at which point the general public\nmay address the Planning Commission on an agenda item by waiting in the City Hall Court Yard and\nentering the Chambers one at a time. No one will be admitted into the Council Chambers who is ill or\nexhibiting symptoms of COVID-19. The Planning Commission is not accepting live comments\nelectronically at this time.\n\nThe public can submit written comments prior to 3:00 p.m. on the day of the meeting in person to the\nPlanning Department at City Hall or via email to dromo@cityofavalon.com.\n\nStaff reports for each agenda item are posted a minimum of 72 hours prior to the meeting and are available\nfor review in person or on the City website at http://www.cityofavalon.com.\n\n  In compliance with the Americans with Disability Act, if you need special assistance to participate in this\n  meeting, please contact Denise Radde, City Clerk (310) 510-0220. Notification 48 hours prior to the meeting\n  will enable the City to make reasonable arrangements to ensure accessibility to this meeting. (28 CFR 35.\n  102-35.104 ADA Title II). All public records relating to an agenda item on this agenda are available for the\n  public inspection at the time the records are distributed to all, or a majority of all, members of the Planning\n  Commission. Such records shall be available at City Hall located at 410 Avalon Canyon Road.\n\nCALL TO ORDER\n\nPLEDGE OF ALLEGIANCE/ROLL CALL\n\nORAL COMMUNICATION: This is the time for any member of the public to address the Planning\nCommission on any matter not on the agenda that is within the subject matter jurisdiction of the City. The\nBrown Act, with limited exceptions, does not allow the Commission or staff to discuss or take action on\nissues brought forth under Public Comment. Concerns may be referred to staff or placed on the next\navailable agenda. Please note that comments from the public will also be taken on each agenda item.\nComments shall be limited to three minutes per person.\n\nCONSENT CALENDAR: All items listed under the Consent Agenda are considered to be routine in\nnature and will be approved by one motion. There will be no separate discussion of these items unless a\nmember of the Commission requests otherwise, in which case the item will be removed for separate\nconsideration. Any item so removed will be taken up following the motion to approve the Consent Agenda.\n\n65059.00002\\40962849.1\n\f  1. Minutes of the December 7, 2022 Special Planning Commission Meeting\n  Recommended Action: Approve and file in addition to the official live recording.\n  2. Resolution 22-48 Request for approval of a Conditional Use Permit (amendment), Coastal\n  Development Permit, and Site Plan application for the expansion of the outdoor patio footprint\n  associated with an existing restaurant “Antonio’s Cabaret.” The project is exempt from\n  environmental review under CEQA Section 15303 - New Construction or Conversion of Small\n  Structures. The project is in the Coastal Zone and is subject to a Coastal Development Permit,\n  which requires a public hearing and Planning Commission approval. The Planning Commission\n  will determine whether the project is within the 300-foot appealable seaward area boundary.\n  Recommended Action:\n  Adopt Resolution 22-48\n\n  3. Resolution 22-30 Request for approval of a\n  Site Plan, Conditional Use Permit and a Coastal Development Permit authorizing the operation\n  of a Beer Garden (AMC 9-6.203 “restaurants, bars and cafes”) within the Commercial “C” zone\n  located Front Street at 301 Crescent Ave. The project is in the Coastal Zone and is subject to a\n  Coastal Development Permit, which requires a public hearing and Planning Commission\n  approval. The Planning Commission will determine whether the project is within the 300-foot\n  appealable seaward area boundary. The project is Categorically Exempt under Section 15303(c)\n  of the State CEQA Guidelines – New Construction or Conversion of Small Structures.\n  Recommended Action:\n  Adopt Resolution 22-30\n\nGENERAL BUSINESS:\n\n  4. An application for a Site Plan for an 85 square foot infill addition to an existing single family\n  dwelling in the Low Density Residential zone. The Planning Commission will consider whether\n  the project is in the Coastal Zone but exempt from a Coastal Development Permit, as well as State\n  CEQA guidelines per Section 15301 - Existing facilities. The Planning Commission will\n  determine whether the project is within the 300-foot appealable seaward area boundary. The\n  associated entitlements require Planning Commission review but no public hearing.\n  Location: 345 Eucalyptus; APN: 7480-033-009 Owner/Applicant: Fineline\n\n  PUBLIC HEARING:\n\n  5. (Continued from 12/07/2022) Staff Recommends the Planning Commission Continue on the\n  Record a Revocation of a Conditional Use Permit/Transient Rental License to the Regular\n  February Planning Commission Meeting allowing the renting or leasing of a room or rooms for\n  periods of fewer than thirty (30) consecutive days duration, and consider whether the project is\n  Categorically Exempt under CEQA Section 15321 (Enforcement Actions by Regulatory Agencies).\n  Location: 5 Camino de Flores #46; APN: 7480-049-062 Owner: Anderson Jeffrey Co Tr Anderson\n  Family Trust\n\n  6. (Continued from 12/07/2022) Staff Recommends the Planning Commission Continue on the\n  Record a Revocation of a Conditional Use Permit/Transient Rental License to the Regular\n  February Planning Commission Meeting allowing the renting or leasing of a room or rooms for\n  periods of fewer than thirty (30) consecutive days duration, and consider whether the project is\n  Categorically Exempt under CEQA Section 15321 (Enforcement Actions by Regulatory Agencies).\n  Location: 81 Gaviota (Unit 161); APN 7480-047-044 Owner: Rushing Robert\n\n65059.00002\\40962849.1\n\f  7. (Continued from 12/07/2022) Staff Recommends the Planning Commission Continue on the\n  Record a Revocation of a Conditional Use Permit/Transient Rental License to the Regular\n  February Planning Commission Meeting allowing the renting or leasing of a room or rooms for\n  periods of fewer than thirty (30) consecutive days duration, and consider whether the project is\n  Categorically Exempt under CEQA Section 15321 (Enforcement Actions by Regulatory Agencies).\n  Location 200 Claressa; APN 7480-011-009 Owner: McKinnon David\n\n  8. Variance(s) and Site Plan application for a minor residential remodel/addition, and consider\n  whether exempt from environmental review in accordance with CEQA Section 15301 - Existing\n  Facilities. 2831\n  Location: 327 Eucalyptus Ave; APN 7480-033-009\n  Owner/Applicant: Vawter Betty L Betty Vawter Trust; Rep. Straight Up Builders\n\n  9. Transient Rental License and Coastal Development Permit to allow the renting or leasing of a\n  room or rooms for periods of fewer than 30 consecutive days, and consider whether Categorically\n  Exempt under CEQA Section 15305 – Minor Alterations in Land Use Limitations.\n  Location: 335 Wrigley Road ; APN: 7480-007-044 Owner/Applicant: Eric Albert\n\nPLANNING DEPARTMENT / CITY ATTORNEY / COMMISSIONER REPORTS\n\nADJOURN\n\nAnyone may appeal any decision made by the Commission to the City Council. To appeal, you must file a\nwritten appeal, and include an appeal fee of $1027.00 with the City Clerk within fifteen (15) calendar days\nof such action. Any person challenging actions of the Planning Commission may be limited to issues he or\nsomeone else raises in writing prior to or orally at the hearing. Any person may appeal the Planning\nCommission’s determination that a proposed project is seaward of the appealable seaward area boundary\nto the Coastal Commission, and following exhaustion of local appeals, any aggrieved person may appeal\nactions on local Coastal Development Permits seaward of the appealable area boundary to the Coastal\nCommission.\n\nBy: Donna Terpinitz-Romo  Posted: City Hall Friday, January 13, 2023\nAdministrative Assistant Building and Planning  Website Friday, January 13, 2023\n\n65059.00002\\40962849.1\n\f  AVALON PLANNING COMMISSION MEETING\n  WEDNESDAY DECEMBER 7, 2022\n  MINUTES\n\nCALL TO ORDER-5:00 p.m.\n\nROLL CALL- Chairman Bart Glass, Commissioners Eric Huart, Yolanda Say, Sandra Putnam,\nRobert Kennedy, and Alternate Commissioner Armida Ruth. Also present: City Manager David\nMaistros, City Attorney Scott Campbell, Code Enforcement Attorney William Litback (Via\nZoom), Senior Administrative Assistant Donna Terpinitz-Romo, Assistant Planner Susanna\nGutierrez and AV Specialist and Director of Accounting Matt Baker.\nAbsent: Vice Chairperson Kathy Smith and Commissioner Jessica Romero\n\nCONSENT CALENDAR- One item on the consent calendar.\n\n1.  Minutes\n  Motion to approve Minutes from the November 16, 2022 Planning Commission Meeting\n  made by Commissioner Kennedy and seconded by Alt. Commissioner Ruth.\n  (5 Ayes: Glass, Putnam, Kennedy, Huart and Ruth, and 2 Absent: Smith and Romero\n  1 Abstain: Say)\n\nPUBLIC HEARING\n\n 2.  150 Middle Terrace\n  This item was continued from the Planning Commission Meeting on November 16, 2022.\n  Public hearing was opened, no comments were received from the public, Public Hearing\n  Closed.\n  Motion to approve with Staff recommendation made by Commissioner Huart, seconded\n  by Commissioner Kennedy. (6 Ayes: Glass, Say, Putnam, Kennedy, Huart and Ruth,\n  2 Absent: Romero and Smith)\n\n   Found the project categorically exempt under CEQA Section 15301, Class 1 –\n  Existing Facilities, subsection (e) (1)\n   Found the application exempt from Coastal Development Permit and make the\n  determination that the project is not within the 300-foot appealable zone.\n   Approved the Site Plan as proposed with all applicable entitlements incorporated,\n  subject to the standard list of conditions (attached as Exhibit A to Attachment 1).\n   Adopted the findings to support the issuance of a setback variance.\n   Found the application allowable under the 2022 Growth Policy.\n\n3.  67 Playa Azul\n  This item was continued from the Planning Commission Meeting on November 16, 2022.\n  Chairman Glass recused himself due to location proximity.\n\fPLANNING COMMISSION MINUTES\nDECEMBER 7, 2022\nPAGE 2\n\n  Public hearing was opened, comments were heard from the applicant and several\n  members of the audience, and then the Public Hearing was closed by Commissioner\n  Huart. Motion to approve Staff recommendations and to include a six month saturation\n  study on Hamilton Cove was made by Commissioner Kennedy and seconded\n  Commissioner Say.\n  (4 Ayes: Say, Kennedy, Putnam and Huart, No: Ruth, 2 Absent: Romero and Smith, 1\n  Abstain: Glass)\n\n   Found the project categorically exempt under CEQA Section 15301, Class 1 –\n  Existing Facilities, subsection (e) (1)\n   Found the application exempt from Coastal Development Permit and make the\n  determination that the project is within the 300-foot appealable zone.\n   Approved the findings necessary the transient rental license ; and\n   Found the application allowable under the 2022 Growth Policy.\n   Included a six month saturation study of Hamilton Cove Conditional Use Permits\n\n4.  108 Middle Terrace\n  This item was continued from the Planning Commission Meeting on November 16, 2022.\n  Chairman Glass opened the Public Hearing, comments were heard from the audience, and\n  then the Public Hearing was closed. Motion to approve Staff recommendations made by\n  Commissioner Huart and seconded by Commissioner Kennedy.\n  (5 Ayes: Glass, Kennedy, Huart, Say and Ruth, 2 Absent: Romero and Smith)\n\n   Found the project categorically exempt under CEQA Section 15301, Class 1 –\n  Existing Facilities, subsection (e) (1)\n   Found the application exempt from Coastal Development Permit and make the\n  determination that the project is within the 300-foot appealable zone.\n   Approved the findings necessary the transient rental license ; and\n   Found the application allowable under the 2022 Growth Policy.\n\n5.  93 Playa Azul\n  Chairman Glass recused himself due to location proximity. Public Hearing was opened,\n  comments were received from the applicant and several members of the audience, and\n  then the Public Hearing was closed by Commissioner Huart. Motion to approve with\n  Staff recommendations and then amended to include a six month saturation study was\n  made by Commissioner Kennedy seconded by Commissioner Putnam.\n  (4 Ayes: Kennedy, Huart, Say, and Putnam, 1 No: Ruth, 2 Absent Romero and Smith\n  1 Abstain: Glass)\n\n   Found the project categorically exempt under CEQA Section 15301, Class 1 –\n  Existing Facilities, subsection (e) (1)\n\fPLANNING COMMISSION MINUTES\nDECEMBER 7, 2022\nPAGE 3\n\n   Found the application exempt from Coastal Development Permit and make the\n  determination that the project is within the 300-foot appealable zone.\n   Approved the findings necessary the transient rental license ; and\n   Found the application allowable under the 2022 Growth Policy.\n   Included a six month saturation study of Hamilton Cove Conditional Use Permits\n\n6.  320 Catalina Avenue\n  Public Hearing was opened, comments were heard from the home owner and the\n  audience, and then the Public Hearing was closed by Chairman Glass. Motion to approve\n  Staff recommendations made by Commissioner Kennedy and seconded by Commissioner\n  Huart.\n  (6 Ayes- Glass, Kennedy, Huart, Say and Ruth, and Putnam 2 Absent: Romero and\n  Smith)\n\n   Found the project categorically exempt under CEQA Section 15301, Class 1 –\n  Existing Facilities, subsection (e) (1)\n   Found the application exempt from Coastal Development Permit and make the\n  determination that the project is within the 300-foot appealable zone.\n   Approved the findings necessary the transient rental license ; and\n   Found the application allowable under the 2022 Growth Policy.\n\n7.  86 Gaviota\n  Chairman Glass recused himself due to location proximity. Public Hearing was opened,\n  comments were received from the applicant and several members of the audience, and\n  then the Public Hearing was closed by Commissioner Huart. Motion to approve with\n  Staff recommendations and to include a six month saturation study was made by\n  Commissioner Kennedy seconded by Commissioner Say.\n  (4 Ayes: Kennedy, Huart, Say, and Putnam 1 No: Ruth, 1 Abstain: Glass, 2 Absent: Romero\n  and Smith)\n\n   Found the project categorically exempt under CEQA Section 15301, Class 1 –\n  Existing Facilities, subsection (e) (1)\n   Found the application exempt from Coastal Development Permit and make the\n  determination that the project is within the 300-foot appealable zone.\n   Approved the findings necessary the transient rental license ; and\n   Found the application allowable under the 2022 Growth Policy\n   Included a six month saturation study of Hamilton Cove Conditional Use Permits\n\fPLANNING COMMISSION MINUTES\nDECEMBER 7, 2022\nPAGE 4\n\n8.  Revocation: 5 Camino de Flores, 81 Gaviota & 200 Claressa\n  Prior to the hearing homeowners requested to cure the CUP’s belonging to these\n  properties therefore a motion was made by Commissioner Huart to continue this item to\n  the January 18, Planning Commission Meeting, seconded by Commissioner Kennedy.\n  (5 Ayes: Kennedy, Huart, Say, Putnam and Ruth, 1 Abstain: Glass, 2 Absent: Romero and\n  Smith)\n\n9.  Revocations: 70 Playa Azul and 201 East Whittley\n  These two items were pulled from the agenda. 70 Playa Azul was pulled due to an\n  administrative error and 201 E Whittley is under construction and unable to be rented.\n\n10.  Revocations: 77 Playa Azul, 208 Descanso, 216 Marilla and 335 Eucalyptus\n  Motion was made for recommendation to City Council for revocation of the CUP’s from\n  these properties based on nonpayment of TOT’s and no active City of Avalon Business\n  Licenses by Commissioner Kennedy seconded by Commissioner Say.\n  (5 Ayes: Kennedy, Say, Huart, Putnam and Ruth, 1 Abstain: Glass, 2 Absent: Smith and\n  Romero)\n\n   Found findings necessary to revoke CUP Following one year of non-\n  use/non-conformance in accordance with Avalon Municipal Code section\n  9-8.503(b) and (c)\n   Determined that the project is Categorically Exempt pursuant to CEQA\n  Section 15321 (Enforcement Actions by Regulatory Agencies)\n\nChairman Glass adjourned the meeting at 7:03 p.m.\n\nI, Donna Terpinitz-Romo, Senior Administrative Assistant, do hereby certify that the DVD\nvideotape of the Special Planning Commission Meeting on December 7, 2022 is the official\nrecord of the Planning Commission Meeting and is on file and maintained in City Hall.\n\n______________________________________________________\nDonna Terpinitz-Romo/Senior Administrative Assistant\n\fAttachment 1: Resolution No. 22-48\n\n  A RESOLUTION OF THE PLANNING COMMISSION OF THE\n  CITY OF AVALON APPROVING AND ISSUING A\n  CONDITIONAL USE PERMIT, COASTAL DEVELOPMENT\n  PERMIT AND SITE PLAN FOR A RESTAURANT OPERATION\n  AT 302 CRESCENT AVENUE; APN 7480-021-007\n\n  WHEREAS, the Santa Catalina Island Company (“Applicant”) filed an application\n(“Application”) for the approval of a Site Plan, Conditional Use Permit, and Coastal Development\nPermit regarding the expansion of the outdoor patio footprint associated with an existing restaurant\n“Antonio’s Cabaret” located at (“Site”); 302 Crescent Avenue, Avalon, California, further\ndescribed as Assessor’s Parcel Number 7480-017-005, in the Resort Recreational Zone; and\n\n  WHEREAS, This Application for the conditionally allowed usage of the site was executed\nin accordance with Avalon Municipal Code (“AMC”) Section 9-8.102, 9-8.202 & 9-8.403; and\n\n  WHEREAS, on November 16, 2022, the Planning Commission of the City of Avalon\n(“Planning Commission”) conducted a duly noticed public hearing at a regularly scheduled\nmeeting considering the Application at which time all persons wishing to testify in connection\nwith the Application were heard and the Application was comprehensively reviewed and continued\nto the next regularly scheduled meeting; and\n\n  WHEREAS, pursuant to Title 14, California Code of Regulations, section 15367, the City\nof Avalon is the lead agency for purposes of the Project; and\n\n  WHEREAS, The project is Categorically Exempt under Section 15303(c) of the State CEQA\nGuidelines – New Construction or Conversion of Small Structures.\n\n  “A store, motel, office, restaurant or similar structure not involving the use of significant amounts\n  of hazardous substances, and not exceeding 2500 square feet in floor area.”\n\n  WHEREAS, the Planning Commission has considered the staff report, supporting documents,\npublic testimony, and all pertinent information submitted.\n\n  WHEREAS, all other legal prerequisites for the consideration of the Conditional Use\nPermit, and Coastal Development Permit have occurred.\n\n  NOW, THEREFORE, the Planning Commission of the City of Avalon does hereby\ndetermine and find as follows:\n\n  Section 1. All Recitals shall be incorporated into this Resolution by reference. Pursuant to\nSection AMC 9-8.203, and based on the entire record before the Planning Commission, all written\nand oral evidence presented, and the findings set forth in this Resolution, the Planning Commission\ngrants a Site Plan, subject to the conditions of approval set forth in the attached Exhibit “A.”\n\n  1\n\f  (a) Every use, development of land and application of development standards shall take place in\n  compliance with all applicable provisions of this title. The proposed patio is an accessory to the\n  existing restaurant operation, which is a conditionally allowed use in the Resort Recreational zone.\n  The project will comply with all applicable development standards (subject to Commercial “C”\n  criteria). This project does not contribute to maximum height, FAR, or occupation of setbacks (0’\n  required).\n\n  (b) Every use, development of land and application of development standards shall be considered on\n  the basis of the suitability of the site for the particular use or development in-tended, and the\n  total development, including the application of prescribed development standards, shall be so\n  arranged as to avoid traffic congestion, insure the protection of public health, safety and general\n  welfare, prevent adverse effects on neighboring property and shall be in general accord with all\n  elements of the General Plan. There is general accord with the General Plan and adverse\n  effects on neighboring properties are limited as follows: Surrounding properties are developed\n  with mostly visitor serving uses. The site marks the general transition from the high intensity\n  commercial corridor of Front Street to the more passive, open space recreational areas along the\n  shorefront leading up to the casino. The outdoor dining has been operational since the onset of the\n  pandemic, and the property owners would like to formally incorporate the area into their existing\n  footprint. No adverse effects have been reported and restaurant use conforms with the underlying\n  zone.\n\n  (c) Every use, development of land, application of development standards shall be considered on the\n  basis of suitable and functional development design, but is not intended that such approval be\n  interpreted to require a particular style or type of architecture. The intent of the design maintains\n  the existing architectural style while minimally impacting the visual corridor beginning at the\n  nearest exterior wall of the two story structure.\n\n  Section 2. Based on the entire record before the Planning Commission, all written and oral\nevidence presented to the Planning Commission, and the findings set forth in this Resolution, the\nPlanning Commission grants a Conditional Use Permit, subject to the conditions of approval set\nforth in the attached Exhibit “A.”\n\n  1. The proposed conditional use shall conform with and carry out the certified Land Use Plan for\n  the area. No change of use is proposed, which is currently conforming. The outdoor dining has\n  been operational since the onset of the pandemic, and the property owners would like to formally\n  incorporate the area into their existing footprint.\n\n  2. The nature, condition, and development of adjacent uses, buildings, and structures shall be\n  considered and no proposed conditional use shall be permitted where such use will adversely\n  affect or be materially detrimental to said adjacent uses, buildings, and structures. The patio\n  expansion would be an accessory use to the restaurant as it currently operates. Outdoor dining, as\n  opposed to a more intense structural occupation, limits massing, energy consumption, and visual\n  impact. The site is not directly adjacent to any residential development.\n\n  3. The site for a proposed conditional use shall be adequate in size and shape to accommodate the\n  yards, walls, fences, parking and loading facilities, landscaping, and other development features\n  prescribed in this chapter or as required as a condition in order to integrate said use with the\n  uses in the neighborhood. The requested Conditional Use Permit does not involve any permanent\n\n  2\n\f  alterations to the site, which is of adequate size and currently unimproved. The current operation\n  utilizes white picket fencing (>50% visibility) and minimal landscaping to identify the patio\n  boundaries.\n\n  4. The site on which the conditional use is located is served by a street that is adequate in width and\n  improved as necessary to carry the kind and quantity of traffic that such use would generate.\n  This project promotes a concentration of use, maximizes visual resources, and is compatible with\n  the design of nearby development. The outdoor patio expansion activates the existing open\n  recreational area and stimulates foot traffic along an adequately sized pedestrian corridor. It also\n  serves as a buffer between high intensity commercial uses on front street and more passive\n  recreational areas along Casino Way.\n\n  5. The proposed conditional use shall be consistent with and carry out the purpose and intent of\n  the underlying zone. The principal use of the R/R zone is that of resort hotel use and associated\n  activities. Standards of development shall be as provided in the \"C\" zone (9-6.404).\n\n  Section 3. Based on the entire record before the Planning Commission, all written and oral\nevidence presented to the Planning Commission, and the findings set forth in this Resolution, the\nPlanning Commission grants a Coastal Development Permit, subject to the conditions of approval\nset forth in the attached Exhibit “A.”\n\n  1. The use is consistent with the goals and objectives of the public access and recreational policies\n  of the California Coastal Act.\n\n  The use is consistent with the following goals and objectives:\n\n  •  Public Access:\n  o Section 30211 “Development shall not interfere with the public's right of access to the sea”\n  o Section 30212 “Public access from the nearest public roadway to the shoreline and along\n  the coast shall be provided in new development projects”\n  o Section 30213 “Lower cost visitor and recreational facilities shall be protected,\n  encouraged, and, where feasible, provided. Developments providing public recreational\n  opportunities are preferred.”\n  •  Recreation:\n  o Section 30222 “The use of private lands suitable for visitor-serving commercial\n  recreational facilities…. shall have priority”\n\n  2. The use is in conformance with the Certified Local Coastal Program for the City of Avalon,\n  which includes the Avalon General Plan, the Local Coastal Plan for Avalon, as well as the\n  Avalon Zoning and Affordable Housing Ordinances.\n  The use conforms to the City of Avalon Local Coastal Program as follows:\n  • General Plan land use designation (Commercial) is consistent, as well as the zoning (Resort\n  Recreation).\n  • Coastal Act Section 30214 “The public access policies of this article shall be implemented in\n  a manner that takes into account the need to regulate the time, place, and manner of public\n\n  3\n\f  access depending on the facts and circumstances in each case including, but not limited to, the\n  following:\n  o (1) Topographic and geologic site characteristics.\n  o (2) The capacity of the site to sustain use and at what level of intensity.\n  o (4) The need to provide for the management of access areas so as to protect the privacy of\n  adjacent property owners and to protect the aesthetic values of the area by providing for\n  the collection of litter.\n\n  3. The use has received all necessary local approvals issuable by the Planning Commission,\n  including Growth Ordinance Pre-Approval (in concept) together with water allocation, as may\n  be required. Upon obtaining approval of the requested entitlements included in this report (CUP\n  modification, CDP and Site Plan), the proposed project will have received all necessary local\n  approvals issuable by the Planning Commission. As follow up, detailed plans will be submitted and\n  reviewed by the Building and Planning Department.\n\n  4. The use has been cleared with respect to environmental regulations of CEQA, as necessary. The\n  proposed use is categorically exempt under CEQA Section 15303 of the State CEQA Guidelines –\n  New Construction or Conversion of Small Structures.\n\n  5. For all development seaward of the nearest public highway to the shoreline, the proposed\n  development conforms to the public access and recreation policies of Chapter 3 of the Coastal\n  Act. The project shall not interfere with the public's right of access to the sea and has been designed\n  to preserve and strengthen the visual and physical connections between the waterfront. The\n  proposal involves the temporary improvement of the site and no physical/structural changes to the\n  building.\n\n  Section 4. Based on the entire record before the Planning Commission and all written and\noral evidence presented to the Planning Commission, the Planning Commission hereby finds the\nnature and extent of the dedications, reservations, impact fees, and other exactions set forth in\nExhibit “A”, if any, are reasonably related to public needs and roughly proportional to the impacts\ncreated by entitlements for the Site.\n\n  Section 5. The Planning Commission’s decision shall become effective fifteen (15) days\nafter the receipt by the Applicant of a written notice of action taken unless an appeal has been\nmade to the City Clerk of Avalon within the said fifteen (15) days.\n\n  Section 6. The Chair of the Planning Commission shall sign this Resolution and the\nSecretary of the Planning Commission shall certify the adoption thereof.\n\n  NOW, THEREFORE BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED by the Planning Commission of\nthe City of Avalon that the Application is hereby approved, subject to the Conditions of\nApproval in Exhibit A, and that a copy of this Resolution be delivered to the City Clerk as well\nas the Applicant.\n\n  PASSED, APPROVED AND ADOPTED this 16th day of November, 2022.\n\n  4\n\f  ________________________\n  Chairman Eric Huart\n\nATTEST:\n\n___________________________\nDave Maistros, City Manager\n\nCERTIFICATE\n\n  I, Donna Terpinitz-Romo, hereby certify that the foregoing Resolution 22-48 was\nadopted by the Planning Commission of the City of Avalon at a regular meeting held on the 16th\nday of November, 2022, by the following vote, to wit:\n\nAYES:\n\nNOES:\n\nABSENT:\n\nABSTAIN:\n\n  __________________________________________\n  Donna Terpinitz-Romo Administrative Assistant\n\n  5\n\f  Exhibit A\n\n  Conditions of Approval\n  Street Address: 302 Crescent Avenue\n  PC# 2822\n\nThe following conditions are specific to PC Application #2822:\n\n1. This project approval shall expire on November 16, 2025 unless a time extension is\n  requested, approved, and filed with the Planning Department prior to expiration.\n\n2. All access roads, yards and parking areas shall be properly maintained for life of the project.\n\n3. The Commission may impose additional conditions as are deemed necessary at the time of\n  discussion to protect the public health, safety and general welfare, including any pertinent\n  factors affecting the establishment, operation or maintenance of the requested use.\n\nThe following conditions require additional action by or documentation from, the applicant\nbefore building permits can be issued:\n\nThe California Department of Fish & Wildlife filing fee shall be submitted as required by\nCalifornia Environmental Quality Act (CEQA) statute, Section 21089(b) and Fish and Game\nCode Section 711.4. The fee should be paid within five (5) days of approval of the environmental\nfiling at the Los Angeles County Clerk’s Office. Once fees have been paid, the applicant shall\nsubmit a copy of all documentation to the City of Avalon, verifying the fees have been paid.\nSaid permit shall not become valid, vested or operative until the fee has been paid.\n\nNo development, including demolition/construction work, shall commence on the subject\nproperty until appropriate permits have been applied for and obtained from City of Avalon\nBuilding  and  Safety  Department.\n\nIf applicable, confirmation is provided by SCE that this project will not require additional fresh\nwater.\n\nProjects resulting in demolition of existing structures or projects exceeding $75,000 in value\nmust provide certification of the condition of the sewer lateral before building permits can be\nissued.\n\nProjects involving structures constructed before 1979 and that requires any demolition, must be\nassessed for the presence of asbestos, lead and other hazardous materials which shall be handled\nin accordance with applicable State codes, South Coast Air Quality Management District\nregulations, as well as the Federal National Emissions Standard for Hazardous Air Pollutants\n(NESHAP). Copies of inspection results and shipping manifests (when disposal is required)\nmust be provided prior to issuance of a demolition permit or building (major renovation) permit.\n\nProjects requiring excavation and/or grading in a mapped archeologically area defined in the\nCultural Resources Management Plan for the City, may be required to have a qualified\n\n  6\n\farcheological monitor onsite during excavation and/or grading.\n\nProjects requiring obstruction of the public right of way must obtain obstruction permits prior to\nor simultaneous with the issuance of a building permit.\n\nProjects requiring excavation in the public right of way must obtain excavation permits prior to\nor simultaneous with the issuance of a building permit.\n\nProjects subject to payment of Long Beach Unified School District fees must provide proof of\npayment or exemption prior to issuance of the building permit.\n\nThe following conditions apply to all projects:\n\nThe project shall be under construction (defined as foundations in and framing in progress)\nwithin 36 months of this approval or all permits will be null and void.\n\nProjects located in the floodplain as defined by the FIRM (Flood Insurance Rate Map), must\nprovide a base flood elevation certificate for the new or substantially improved structure\nresulting from this application before a building permit can be issued.\n\nLegal lot status must be verified in compliance with local and state subdivision laws, including\nthe Subdivision Map Act (“SMA”) prior to the expiration of this project approval. Should any\nland division violation be determined, the applicant may request a Certificate of Compliance to\ncreate a buildable parcel.\n\nIf the City discovers that material facts were not disclosed by applicant or that facts provided by\nthe applicant were different than represented, the City may impose additional conditions of\napproval to mitigate any adverse impacts. The Planning Commission may revoke or modify the\napproval in the future if the Commission finds that the use to which the permit allows is\ndetrimental to health, safety, comfort, general welfare of the public; constitutes a public\nnuisance; if the permit was obtained or is being used by fraud; and/or if one or more the\nconditions upon which a permit was granted are in noncompliance or have been violated.\n\nThe project shall be developed and maintained in strict accordance with the approved plans\nunless written approval is given by the Planning Department.\n\nThis approval does not abridge or supersede the regulatory powers and permit requirements of\nany federal, state, or local agency requirements, which may retain a regulatory or advisory\nfunction as specified by statute or ordinance. Authorization by the Planning Department does\nnot waive the requirement that the project comply with all sections of the Avalon Municipal\ncode and all other relevant local/state/federal regulations. The applicant shall obtain and\nmaintain permits as may be required from each agency.\n\nThe applicant is responsible for ensuring that all project workers are informed of, understand,\nand agree to abide by the approved plans and project conditions.\n\n  7\n\fAll construction on the project shall be limited to Monday through Saturday, 8am to 7pm. No\nconstruction shall occur on Sundays.\n\nAll project contractors and sub-contractors must maintain a City of Avalon business licenses in\ncompliance with all local, state, and federal laws allowing them to work in the City.\n\nNon-combustible roofing materials shall be used subject to approval by the Building and Fire\nDepartments.\n\nApproved fire walls shall be constructed as required by the adopted Building Code.\n\nFire Suppression system is required if the project is for:\n  a) New Structure; or\n  b) Rehabilitation, remodeling or reconstruction of twenty five percent (25%) or more of the\n  gross square footage of an existing commercial or multiple unit residential building or\n  structure; or\n  c) Rehabilitation, remodeling or reconstruction of fifty percent (50%) or more of the gross\n  square footage of a single unit residential building or structure and the cost of the work\n  is more than $10,000.00.\n  d) Change in the use of the building or structure. Change in use is defined as “those\n  alterations which significantly alter the use, increase the number of dwelling units in\n  an existing building or result in a building being held open to the public where the prior\n  use was for private purposes only. This could include changes from principally\n  permitted to conditionally permitted uses, but excludes the conversion of a single\n  family residence from transient to non-transient rental or from non-transient to transient\n  rental. The requirement shall apply to changes from commercial to residential, or\n  residential to commercial, but would not include expansions of existing commercial\n  uses, such as the addition of table service at an existing coffee bar or the addition of\n  service of liquor at a restaurant.”\n  e) Work has been done without City permit(s) and City files do not contain information\n  to enable us to determine extent of changes to building. In order to avoid an order to\n  install a fire sprinkler system, you must provide valid documentation of the structural\n  plan as it existed prior to the un-permitted work.\n\nThe site shall be kept in a neat manner at all times and the landscaping shall be continuously\nmaintained in a healthy and thriving condition. During construction activities, the applicant shall\nremove daily accumulation of mud and dirt from any roads adjacent to the site; any removal of\nvegetation or construction materials shall be lawfully disposed of.\n\nNon-potable water must be used for dust control during construction.\nThe entire sanitation system shall be connected to the City salt water system.\n\nThe design and construction of all sewers shall be approved by the Department of Public Works,\nincluding the design and construction of sewer connections and laterals.\n\nStructural drainage shall be provided by use of roof gutters and downspouts which allow for\n\n  8\n\fproper drainage away from the foundation of any structure and which do not cause any pooling\nof standing water.\n\nAll plans and specifications must be approved by the City of Avalon Building Department prior\nto construction beginning.\n\nCity staff shall review and approve location, size and noise output of any mechanical devices.\n\nAny changes mandated by the City of Avalon Building Department that would require an\namended site plan must be reviewed and approved by the Planning Commission before any\nconstruction takes place.\n\nOwner/Applicant must offer to dedicate to the City the following easements measuring at least\n10 feet in width, if applicable:\n\n  Public utilities easement\n  Water line easement\n  Sewer facilities easement\n  Landscape easement\n  Storm drain easement\n\nAny existing public improvements which are damaged as a result of construction regarding the\nproject shall be repaired or replaced to the satisfaction of City staff at the Owner/Applicant’s\nsole cost before a certificate of occupancy can be issued.\n\nOwner/Applicant agrees to indemnify, defend, and hold the City harmless, with counsel\nreasonably acceptable to the City, from costs and expenses, including attorney fees, incurred by\nthe City or held to be the liability of the City in connection with the City’s defense of its actions\nin any proceeding brought in any State or Federal court challenging the City’s actions with\nrespect to the project. Owner/Applicant understands and acknowledges that the City is under no\nobligation to defend any legal actions challenging the project.\n\nAny preexisting Municipal Code violations at the property may be required, in City’s sole\ndiscretion, to be resolved prior to issuance of a certificate of occupancy, even if such Code\nviolations are unrelated to the project.\n\nProject must comply with all mitigation measures noted in any related environmental\nassessments performed in connection with this project, prior to receiving a certificate of\noccupancy.\n\nAll conditions of approval must be completed to the satisfaction of the City Planning Department\nprior to occupancy of the project.\n\nProject applicant must pay the City all statutory fees under the City’s fee ordinance, unless\notherwise waived by the Director.\n\n  9\n\fAttachment 1: Resolution No. 22-30\n\n  A RESOLUTION OF THE PLANNING COMMISSION OF THE\n  CITY OF AVALON DEFERRING APPROVAL OF A SITE PLAN\n  AND CONDITIONALLY APPROVING AND ISSUING A\n  CONDITIONAL USE PERMIT AND COASTAL DEVELOPMENT\n  PERMIT FOR A RESTAURANT OPERATION AT 310 CRESCENT\n  AVENUE; APN 7480-017-005\n\n  WHEREAS, the Santa Catalina Island Company (“Applicant”) filed an application\n(“Application”) for the approval of a Site Plan, Conditional Use Permit, and Coastal Development\nPermit regarding the establishment of a restaurant operation located at (“Site”); 301 Crescent\nAvenue, Avalon, California, further described as Assessor’s Parcel Number 7480-017-005, in the\nCommercial Zone; and\n\n  WHEREAS, This Application for the proposed conditionally allowed usage of the site was\nexecuted in accordance with Avalon Municipal Code (“AMC”) Section 9-8.102, 9-8.202 & 9-\n8.403; and\n\n  WHEREAS, on August 17, 2022, the Planning Commission of the City of Avalon\n(“Planning Commission”) conducted a duly noticed public hearing at a regularly scheduled\nmeeting considering the Application at which time all persons wishing to testify in connection\nwith the Application were heard and the Application was comprehensively reviewed and continued\nto the next regularly scheduled meeting; and\n\n  WHEREAS, on October 19th, 2022, the Planning Commission held an additional duly-noticed\npublic hearing, at which all those in attendance were given the opportunity to speak; and\n\n  WHEREAS, site plan approval has been deferred to an unspecified date; and\n\n  WHEREAS, the specific buildout of the site shall be reconsidered by the Commission at\na duly noticed public hearing to determine compliance with all applicable development standards,\nuntil which point the entitlements approved in this Resolution 22-30 are considered contingent;\n\n  WHEREAS, pursuant to Title 14, California Code of Regulations, section 15367, the City\nof Avalon is the lead agency for purposes of the Project; and\n\n  WHEREAS, The project is Categorically Exempt under Section 15303(c) of the State CEQA\nGuidelines – New Construction or Conversion of Small Structures.\n\n  “A store, motel, office, restaurant or similar structure not involving the use of significant amounts\n  of hazardous substances, and not exceeding 2500 square feet in floor area.”\n\n  WHEREAS, the Planning Commission has considered the staff report, supporting documents,\npublic testimony, and all pertinent information submitted.\n\f  WHEREAS, all other legal prerequisites for the consideration of the Conditional Use\nPermit, and Coastal Development Permit have occurred, other than the above discussed Site Plan\napproval.\n\n  NOW, THEREFORE, the Planning Commission of the City of Avalon does hereby\ndetermine and find as follows:\n\n  Section 1. Approval of the Site Plan is deferred until a later meeting to be determined, and\nSection 2 and Section 3 are conditional upon approval of a Site Plan. All Recitals shall be\nincorporated into this Resolution by reference. Pursuant to Section AMC 9-8.203, and based on\nthe entire record before the Planning Commission, all written and oral evidence presented, the\nPlanning Commission hereby defers the approval of a Site Plan upon which all other entitlements\nare contingent, to be subject to the required findings below:\n\n(a) Every use, development of land and application of development standards shall take place\n  in compliance with all applicable provisions of this title.\n\n(b) Every use, development of land and application of development standards shall be\n  considered on the basis of the suitability of the site for the particular use or development in-\n  tended, and the total development, including the application of prescribed development\n  standards, shall be so arranged as to avoid traffic congestion, insure the protection of public\n  health, safety and general welfare, prevent adverse effects on neighboring property and shall\n  be in general accord with all elements of the General Plan.\n\n(c) Every use, development of land, application of development standards shall be considered on\n  the basis of suitable and functional development design, but is not intended that such\n  approval be interpreted to require a particular style or type of architecture.\n\n  Section 2. Based on the entire record before the Planning Commission, all written and oral\nevidence presented to the Planning Commission, and the findings set forth in this Resolution, the\nPlanning Commission grants a Conditional Use Permit, subject to the conditions of approval set\nforth in the attached Exhibit “A.”\n\nThe proposed conditional use shall conform with and carry out the certified Land Use Plan for the area.\nThe subject property is designated Commercial (intended to “accommodate general retail and commercial\nservices and offices”) in the Avalon General Plan whose proposed development is compliant with the\nfollowing policies:\n\n  •  Concentration of uses and design of development to promote walking and use of transit in\n  lieu of the automobile (II-4)\n  •  Orientation of buildings to maximize opportunities for solar energy use, daylighting, and\n  ventilation (II-4)\n  •  Shading of surface parking, walkways, and plazas (II-4)\n  •  Encourage infill of the City Center area and where appropriate, increase the intensity of\n  development on vacant and underutilized properties, in lieu of expanded development\n  outward into Avalon’s hillsides, natural areas and open space (II-9)\n\f  •  Promote the balanced mixture of retail and service needs within the City Center area for\n  the benefit of citizens and tourists. (II-13)\n  •  Encourage the development of visitor-serving commercial recreational facilities designed\n  to enhance opportunities for coastal recreation (II-17)\n  •  Preserve and strengthen the City Center’s historic status as the center of commercial and\n  tourist activity by encouraging a range of uses that serve both residents and visitors (II-22)\n  •  Develop expanded and new recreational opportunities that will meet the needs of local\n  residents and attract visitors (II-24)\n\nThe nature, condition, and development of adjacent uses, buildings, and structures shall be considered\nand no proposed conditional use shall be permitted where such use will adversely affect or be materially\ndetrimental to said adjacent uses, buildings, and structures. Staff is recommending deferring the Site Plan\napproval as a condition of this use permit until detailed plans are submitted, identifying and properly\nmitigating any potential adverse effects of this development.\n\nThe site for a proposed conditional use shall be adequate in size and shape to accommodate the yards,\nwalls, fences, parking and loading facilities, landscaping, and other development features prescribed in\nthis chapter or as required as a condition in order to integrate said use with the uses in the neighborhood.\nThe requested Conditional Use Permit does not involve any permanent alterations to the site, which is of\nadequate size and currently underdeveloped. Staff is recommending full, detailed\nfencing/landscaping/traffic/parking/loading/signage/wayfinding plans be required as a condition of\napproval and utilized in future consideration of a Site Plan.\n\nThe site on which the conditional use is located is served by a street that is adequate in width and\nimproved as necessary to carry the kind and quantity of traffic that such use would generate.\n\n  •  AMC Section 9-7.605 (Parking Requirements – General Standards of Development)\n  Restaurants, Cafes, Nightclubs, Bars and Other Similar Places):\n  o To be determined by the Commission.\n   No parking or vehicular traffic regulation proposed\n   Primary mode of transport to site expected to be pedestrian\n\n  •  AMC Section 9-7.507 (Adequate Streets - General Standards of Development)\n  o “In addition to the provisions of this article, the officer, Commission or Council\n  granting any change of zone, conditional use permit, or variance may as a condition\n  of the same require the dedication and/or improvement of streets rights-of-way…\n  where the granting of such conditional use permit would otherwise be detrimental\n  to the public peace, health and safety by reason of increased traffic congestion\n  occasioned by the improvement of such property”\n\n  •  General Plan Circulation Element:\n  o Stakeholders indicate, and field observations support, a less-than-desirable mix of\n  autoettes, motor vehicles, pedestrians, and bicycles on streets generally bounded\n  by Crescent Avenue, 3rd Street, Clarissa Avenue, and Metropole Avenue. The\n  pedestrian and bicycle experience could be enhanced by a strategic separation\n  of these modes within this general area.\n\f  While the requested location of this Conditional Use Permit is served by an improved street, the\n  directly adjacent crosswalk (intersection of Crescent and Metropole) and greater surrounding\n  pedestrian area is severely congested and fails to successfully integrate multi-modal transportation\n  between people and automobiles. Application materials also failed to address any impacts from\n  food delivery services, commercial loading, and trash pickup; coordination with surrounding\n  properties in the immediate vicinity is particularly essential in this case due to a reliance on\n  commonly shared spaces/facilities.\n\n  It is staff’s recommendation that the Commission approve the proposed use with the added\n  condition that the applicant perform an engineered traffic study and implement any suggested\n  improvements within 18 months of operation, in order to comply with the following General Plan\n  policies.\n\n  •  Enhance the pedestrian character and pedestrian linkages between the waterfront and City\n  Center (II-8)\n  •  Require all new development to fully mitigate the impacts of additional vehicle trips,\n  through physical and operational improvements as appropriate (VI-6)\n  •  Require appropriate traffic mitigation, including traffic calming measures, in conjunction\n  with new development (Implementation Action)\n  •  Ensure that residents and visitors can walk, bicycle, or use transit to access key Avalon\n  attractions, businesses, and places (VI-7)\n  •  Enhance the pedestrian environment by providing a continuous network of pedestrian\n  facilities and minimizing conflicts between pedestrians and vehicles (VI-9)\n  •  Enhance wayfinding for pedestrians, bicyclists, and transit users (VI-11)\n  •  Require new development to provide sufficient, but not excessive, parking to meet demand.\n  Promote alternative solutions to additional parking when possible (VI-13)\n\nThe proposed conditional use shall be consistent with and carry out the purpose and intent of the\nunderlying zone. AMC Section 9-6.102 “The Commercial Zones are established to provide areas in which\nbusiness may be conducted, services rendered, public activities offered, and other such activities as are\nrelated to the function of commercial development.”\n\nAMC Section 9-6.201 “The ‘C’ zone is established to provide areas for the development of certain business\nand professional offices, services and related uses, namely such uses as can conveniently serve the public.”\n\nThe subject property was previously utilized as an “auto, mini-car and golf cart rental” business, allowable\nby Conditional Use Permit in the Commercial “C” Zone and consistent with the General Plan designation.\nThe requested Conditional Use Permit would authorize the operation of a beer garden which falls under the\n“Restaurants, bars and cafes,” classification in the same category (AMC 9-6.203 “Uses by Conditional Use\nPermit”).\n\n  Section 3. Based on the entire record before the Planning Commission, all written and oral\nevidence presented to the Planning Commission, and the findings set forth in this Resolution, the\n\fPlanning Commission grants a Coastal Development Permit, subject to the conditions of approval\nset forth in the attached Exhibit “A.”\n\nThe use is consistent with the goals and objectives of the public access and recreational policies of the\nCalifornia Coastal Act.\n\n  The use is consistent with the following goals and objectives:\n\n  •  Public Access:\n  o Section 30211 “Development shall not interfere with the public's right of access to the sea”\n  o Section 30212 “Public access from the nearest public roadway to the shoreline and along\n  the coast shall be provided in new development projects”\n  o Section 30213 “Lower cost visitor and recreational facilities shall be protected,\n  encouraged, and, where feasible, provided. Developments providing public recreational\n  opportunities are preferred.”\n  •  Recreation:\n  o Section 30222 “The use of private lands suitable for visitor-serving commercial\n  recreational facilities…. shall have priority”\n\nThe use is in conformance with the Certified Local Coastal Program for the City of Avalon, which\nincludes the Avalon General Plan, the Local Coastal Plan for Avalon, as well as the Avalon Zoning and\nAffordable Housing Ordinances.\n\n  The use conforms to the City of Avalon Local Coastal Program as follows:\n  • General Plan land use consistency is found and discussed above in the first finding of the\n  Conditional Use Permit.\n  • Coastal Act Section 30214 “The public access policies of this article shall be implemented in\n  a manner that takes into account the need to regulate the time, place, and manner of public\n  access depending on the facts and circumstances in each case including, but not limited to, the\n  following:\n  o (1) Topographic and geologic site characteristics.\n  o (2) The capacity of the site to sustain use and at what level of intensity.\n  o (4) The need to provide for the management of access areas so as to protect the privacy of\n  adjacent property owners and to protect the aesthetic values of the area by providing for\n  the collection of litter.\n  • Compliance with Commercial Development Standards as per AMC Title 9: contingent upon\n  site plan approval.\n\nThe use has received all necessary local approvals issuable by the Planning Commission, including\nGrowth Ordinance Pre-Approval (in concept) together with water allocation, as may be required. This\nfinding is contingent upon approval of the companion CUP application and the finalized site plan, which\nrequires “the use shall be in general accord with all elements of the General Plan (which includes the Local\nCoastal Plan in its entirety).”\n\fThe use has been cleared with respect to environmental regulations of CEQA, as necessary. The proposed\nuse is categorically exempt under CEQA Section 15303 of the State CEQA Guidelines – New Construction\nor Conversion of Small Structures.\n\nFor all development seaward of the nearest public highway to the shoreline, the proposed development\nconforms to the public access and recreation policies of Chapter 3 of the Coastal Act. The project shall\nnot interfere with the public's right of access to the sea, as discussed in section subsection one. Additionally,\nthe development promotes access as is a recreational beer garden that allows for easy pedestrian access to\nthe coast.\n\n  Section 4. Based on the entire record before the Planning Commission and all written and\noral evidence presented to the Planning Commission, the Planning Commission hereby finds the\nnature and extent of the dedications, reservations, impact fees, and other exactions set forth in\nExhibit “A”, if any, are reasonably related to public needs and roughly proportional to the impacts\ncreated by entitlements for the Site.\n\n  Section 5. The project is Categorically Exempt under Section 15303(c) of the State CEQA\n  Guidelines – New Construction or Conversion of Small Structures.\n\n  “A store, motel, office, restaurant or similar structure not involving the use of significant amounts\n  of hazardous substances, and not exceeding 2500 square feet in floor area.”\n\n  This CEQA exemption applies because this project does not exceed 2500 square feet in floor area\n  and involves only X square feet in floor area.\n\n  Section 6. The Planning Commission’s decision shall become effective fifteen (15) days\nafter the receipt by the Applicant of a written notice of action taken unless an appeal has been\nmade to the City Clerk of Avalon within the said fifteen (15) days.\n\n  Section 7. The Chair of the Planning Commission shall sign this Resolution and the\nSecretary of the Planning Commission shall certify the adoption thereof.\n\n  NOW, THEREFORE BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED by the Planning Commission of\nthe City of Avalon that the Application is hereby approved, subject to the Conditions of\nApproval in Exhibit A, and that a copy of this Resolution be delivered to the City Clerk as well\nas the Applicant.\n\n  PASSED, APPROVED AND ADOPTED this 18th day of January, 2023.\n\n  ________________________\n  Chairman Bart Glass\n\nATTEST:\n\f___________________________\nDave Maistros, City Manager\n\nCERTIFICATE\n\n  I, Donna Terpinitz-Romo, hereby certify that the foregoing Resolution 22-30was\nadopted by the Planning Commission of the City of Avalon at a regular meeting held on the 18th\nday of January, 2023, by the following vote, to wit:\n\nAYES:\n\nNOES:\n\nABSENT:\n\nABSTAIN:\n\n  __________________________________________\n  Donna Terpinitz-Romo Administrative Assistant\n\f  Exhibit A\n\n  Conditions of Approval\n  Street Address: 301 Crescent Avenue\n  PC# 2804\n\nThe following conditions are specific to PC Application #2804:\n\n1. This project approval shall expire on October 19, 2025 unless a time extension is requested,\n  approved, and filed with the Planning Department prior to expiration.\n\n2. All access roads, yards and parking areas shall be properly maintained for life of the project.\n\n3. All applicable entitlements shall be considered inactive until and contingent upon site plan\n  approval at an undetermined future date by the Commission\n\n4. The Commission may impose additional conditions as are deemed necessary at the time of\n  discussion to protect the public health, safety and general welfare, including any pertinent\n  factors affecting the establishment, operation or maintenance of the requested use.\n\nThe following conditions require additional action by or documentation from, the applicant\nbefore building permits can be issued:\n\nThe California Department of Fish & Wildlife filing fee shall be submitted as required by\nCalifornia Environmental Quality Act (CEQA) statute, Section 21089(b) and Fish and Game\nCode Section 711.4. The fee should be paid within five (5) days of approval of the environmental\nfiling at the Los Angeles County Clerk’s Office. Once fees have been paid, the applicant shall\nsubmit a copy of all documentation to the City of Avalon, verifying the fees have been paid.\nSaid permit shall not become valid, vested or operative until the fee has been paid.\n\nNo development, including demolition/construction work, shall commence on the subject\nproperty until appropriate permits have been applied for and obtained from City of Avalon\n\fBuilding  and  Safety  Department.\n\nIf applicable, confirmation is provided by SCE that this project will not require additional fresh\nwater.\n\nProjects resulting in demolition of existing structures or projects exceeding $75,000 in value\nmust provide certification of the condition of the sewer lateral before building permits can be\nissued.\n\nProjects involving structures constructed before 1979 and that requires any demolition, must be\nassessed for the presence of asbestos, lead and other hazardous materials which shall be handled\nin accordance with applicable State codes, South Coast Air Quality Management District\nregulations, as well as the Federal National Emissions Standard for Hazardous Air Pollutants\n(NESHAP). Copies of inspection results and shipping manifests (when disposal is required)\nmust be provided prior to issuance of a demolition permit or building (major renovation) permit.\n\nProjects requiring excavation and/or grading in a mapped archeologically area defined in the\nCultural Resources Management Plan for the City, may be required to have a qualified\narcheological monitor onsite during excavation and/or grading.\n\nProjects requiring obstruction of the public right of way must obtain obstruction permits prior to\nor simultaneous with the issuance of a building permit.\n\nProjects requiring excavation in the public right of way must obtain excavation permits prior to\nor simultaneous with the issuance of a building permit.\n\nProjects subject to payment of Long Beach Unified School District fees must provide proof of\npayment or exemption prior to issuance of the building permit.\n\nThe following conditions apply to all projects:\n\nThe project shall be under construction (defined as foundations in and framing in progress)\nwithin 36 months of this approval or all permits will be null and void.\n\nProjects located in the floodplain as defined by the FIRM (Flood Insurance Rate Map), must\nprovide a base flood elevation certificate for the new or substantially improved structure\nresulting from this application before a building permit can be issued.\n\nLegal lot status must be verified in compliance with local and state subdivision laws, including\nthe Subdivision Map Act (“SMA”) prior to the expiration of this project approval. Should any\nland division violation be determined, the applicant may request a Certificate of Compliance to\ncreate a buildable parcel.\n\nIf the City discovers that material facts were not disclosed by applicant or that facts provided by\nthe applicant were different than represented, the City may impose additional conditions of\napproval to mitigate any adverse impacts. The Planning Commission may revoke or modify the\n\fapproval in the future if the Commission finds that the use to which the permit allows is\ndetrimental to health, safety, comfort, general welfare of the public; constitutes a public\nnuisance; if the permit was obtained or is being used by fraud; and/or if one or more the\nconditions upon which a permit was granted are in noncompliance or have been violated.\n\nThe project shall be developed and maintained in strict accordance with the approved plans\nunless written approval is given by the Planning Department.\n\nThis approval does not abridge or supersede the regulatory powers and permit requirements of\nany federal, state, or local agency requirements, which may retain a regulatory or advisory\nfunction as specified by statute or ordinance. Authorization by the Planning Department does\nnot waive the requirement that the project comply with all sections of the Avalon Municipal\ncode and all other relevant local/state/federal regulations. The applicant shall obtain and\nmaintain permits as may be required from each agency.\n\nThe applicant is responsible for ensuring that all project workers are informed of, understand,\nand agree to abide by the approved plans and project conditions.\n\nAll construction on the project shall be limited to Monday through Saturday, 8am to 7pm. No\nconstruction shall occur on Sundays.\n\nAll project contractors and sub-contractors must maintain a City of Avalon business licenses in\ncompliance with all local, state, and federal laws allowing them to work in the City.\n\nNon-combustible roofing materials shall be used subject to approval by the Building and Fire\nDepartments.\n\nApproved fire walls shall be constructed as required by the adopted Building Code.\n\nFire Suppression system is required if the project is for:\n  a) New Structure; or\n  b) Rehabilitation, remodeling or reconstruction of twenty five percent (25%) or more of the\n  gross square footage of an existing commercial or multiple unit residential building or\n  structure; or\n  c) Rehabilitation, remodeling or reconstruction of fifty percent (50%) or more of the gross\n  square footage of a single unit residential building or structure and the cost of the work\n  is more than $10,000.00.\n  d) Change in the use of the building or structure. Change in use is defined as “those\n  alterations which significantly alter the use, increase the number of dwelling units in\n  an existing building or result in a building being held open to the public where the prior\n  use was for private purposes only. This could include changes from principally\n  permitted to conditionally permitted uses, but excludes the conversion of a single\n  family residence from transient to non-transient rental or from non-transient to transient\n  rental. The requirement shall apply to changes from commercial to residential, or\n  residential to commercial, but would not include expansions of existing commercial\n\f  uses, such as the addition of table service at an existing coffee bar or the addition of\n  service of liquor at a restaurant.”\n  e) Work has been done without City permit(s) and City files do not contain information\n  to enable us to determine extent of changes to building. In order to avoid an order to\n  install a fire sprinkler system, you must provide valid documentation of the structural\n  plan as it existed prior to the un-permitted work.\n\nThe site shall be kept in a neat manner at all times and the landscaping shall be continuously\nmaintained in a healthy and thriving condition. During construction activities, the applicant shall\nremove daily accumulation of mud and dirt from any roads adjacent to the site; any removal of\nvegetation or construction materials shall be lawfully disposed of.\n\nNon-potable water must be used for dust control during construction.\nThe entire sanitation system shall be connected to the City salt water system.\n\nThe design and construction of all sewers shall be approved by the Department of Public Works,\nincluding the design and construction of sewer connections and laterals.\n\nStructural drainage shall be provided by use of roof gutters and downspouts which allow for\nproper drainage away from the foundation of any structure and which do not cause any pooling\nof standing water.\n\nAll plans and specifications must be approved by the City of Avalon Building Department prior\nto construction beginning.\n\nCity staff shall review and approve location, size and noise output of any mechanical devices.\n\nAny changes mandated by the City of Avalon Building Department that would require an\namended site plan must be reviewed and approved by the Planning Commission before any\nconstruction takes place.\n\nOwner/Applicant must offer to dedicate to the City the following easements measuring at least\n10 feet in width, if applicable:\n\n  Public utilities easement\n  Water line easement\n  Sewer facilities easement\n  Landscape easement\n  Storm drain easement\n\nAny existing public improvements which are damaged as a result of construction regarding the\nproject shall be repaired or replaced to the satisfaction of City staff at the Owner/Applicant’s\nsole cost before a certificate of occupancy can be issued.\n\nOwner/Applicant agrees to indemnify, defend, and hold the City harmless, with counsel\nreasonably acceptable to the City, from costs and expenses, including attorney fees, incurred by\n\fthe City or held to be the liability of the City in connection with the City’s defense of its actions\nin any proceeding brought in any State or Federal court challenging the City’s actions with\nrespect to the project. Owner/Applicant understands and acknowledges that the City is under no\nobligation to defend any legal actions challenging the project.\n\nAny preexisting Municipal Code violations at the property may be required, in City’s sole\ndiscretion, to be resolved prior to issuance of a certificate of occupancy, even if such Code\nviolations are unrelated to the project.\n\nProject must comply with all mitigation measures noted in any related environmental\nassessments performed in connection with this project, prior to receiving a certificate of\noccupancy.\n\nAll conditions of approval must be completed to the satisfaction of the City Planning Department\nprior to occupancy of the project.\n\nProject applicant must pay the City all statutory fees under the City’s fee ordinance, unless\notherwise waived by the Director.\n\f  AVALON PLANNING COMMISSION\n  STAFF REPORT – PC# 2830\n  Wednesday, January 18, 2023\n\n  Application File: Site Plan\n  Submitted by: Susanna Gutierrez Assistant Planner\nItem No. – General Business\nProject Title: Application for Site Plan for Interior Remodel at 345 Eucalyptus Avenue\n\nProject Description: An application for a Site Plan for the interior remodel of an existing single\nfamily dwelling, converting living space to a bedroom, in the HDR (flats) zone. The project is in\nthe Coastal Zone but exempt from a Coastal Development Permit, as well as CEQA per Section\n15301 - Existing facilities. The Planning Commission will determine whether the project is within\nthe 300-foot appealable seaward area boundary. The associated entitlements require Planning\nCommission review but no public hearing.\n\n  Owner/Applicant:  Fineline\n  Location:  345 Eucalyptus Ave\n  APN:  7480-033-017\n  PC#:  2830\n  Zone:  High Density Residential (HDR)\n  File Date:  11-30-2022\n\nPage 1 of 12\n\f  BACKGROUND\nPrevious review:\n\nPlanning Commission, January 20, 2016 PC # 2598\n\nA Public Hearing on an application for a Site Plan Review, Local Coastal Development Permit,\nand Resolution to allow the construction of a 1,338 square foot, two-story single-family residence\nat 345 Eucalyptus.\n\nDiscussion:\n\nThis application, which approves the addition of one bedroom via conversion of existing interior\nspace reflects the original intent of the structure’s design - a two story, four bedroom single family\ndwelling totaling 1338 square feet (authorized by PC #2598). No changes to the number of\nbathrooms, number of stories, or use as a single family residence would occur.\n\nPage 2 of 12\n\f  GENERAL PLAN, LAND USE, AND ZONING COMPLIANCE\n\nAnnual Growth Policy:\nStaff recommends that the Planning Commission make the finding as required by the 2022\nGrowth Policy that the proposed remodel will not have any growth inducing effect and does not\ncreate the need for additional employees, thus the project is allowed by the 2022 Growth Policy.\nAs residential use intensity is measured by dwelling units/acre, and the existing density is not\nchanging, there should be no change in intensity. The growth policy provides that new\ndevelopment will only be permitted for the following classes of projects and almost all require\nconfirmation from Southern California Edison Company (“SCE”) that adequate fresh water is\navailable (note - received):\n  1. The repair, in-kind replacement, remodeling or enlargement of existing commercial\n  and residential structures, subject to specified conditions;\n\nSite Plan\n\nA site plan approval is required since the project doesn’t qualify as a “minor renovation” per\nmunicipal code section 9-3.613(b). AMC Section 9-8.203 requires that the Site Plan be approved\nbased upon three (3) factors and principles. These criteria and the corresponding explanation of\nproject compliance are as follows:\n\n  (a) Every use, development of land and application of development standards shall take\n  place in compliance with all applicable provisions of this title.\n\n  The proposed project does not have any foreseeable effect on the use or development\n  standards associated with this property. As the intensity of residential use is measured\n  by dwelling units/acre, and the existing density (1 unit – SFR) is not changing, there\n  should be no change in intensity. Bedroom count is not a contributing factor to this\n  calculation.\n\n  (b) Every use, development of land and application of development standards shall be\n  considered on the basis of the suitability of the site for the particular use or\n  development in-tended, and the total development, including the application of\n  prescribed development standards, shall be so arranged as to avoid traffic\n  congestion, insure the protection of public health, safety and general welfare, prevent\n  adverse effects on neighboring property and shall be in general accord with all\n  elements of the General Plan.\n\n  As discussed, the scope of work is limited to internal reconfiguration within an existing\n  structure, which should not have an effect on neighboring properties or its accordance\n  with the General Plan. The applicant will need to follow standard building permit\n  requirements and meet all applicable codes.\n\n  (c) Every use, development of land, application of development standards shall be\n  considered on the basis of suitable and functional development design, but is not\n\nPage 3 of 12\n\f  intended that such approval be interpreted to require a particular style or type of\n  architecture.\n\n  The design of the proposed remodel is suitable for a single family residential structure\n  and will have minimal effect on the architectural style of the building. The addition of\n  one bedroom via conversion of existing interior space reflects the original intent of the\n  design, a two story, four bedroom single family dwelling totaling 1338 square feet. No\n  changes to the number of bathrooms, number of stories, or use as a single family\n  residence would occur.\n\n  ENVIRONMENTAL DETERMINATION\n\nCEQA:\n\nThe project is Categorically Exempt under Section 15301(a) CEQA Guidelines-Existing\nFacilities, which exempts the following:\n  (a) Interior or exterior alterations involving such things as interior partitions, plumbing, and\n  electrical conveyances;\n\nCoastal Development:\n  All projects within the City of Avalon are located\n  in the Coastal Zone. Under AMC 9-8.103(h), the\n  Planning Commission must determine whether the\n  project is within the 300 foot appealable seaward\n  area boundary. Based on the appealable area map\n  excerpt included, it is staff’s recommendation that\n  the Planning Commission make the finding that the\n  project is not within the 300 foot appealable zone.\n  City staff determined the project to be exempt from\n  a Coastal Development Permit according to\n  Municipal Code section 9-8.102(b)(1), as an\n  “improvement to an existing single-family\n  residence” that does not fall into any of the\n  subcategories “involving a risk of adverse\n  environment effect.”\n\nFresh Water Availability (SCE): Staff has received confirmation from SCE authorizing the\ndescribed scope of work and finding fresh water availability.\n\nRecommendations: Based on the findings, Staff recommends the Planning Commission:\n\n  1. Find the project categorically exempt under CEQA § 15301, Class 1 – Existing Facilities.\n  2. Find the application exempt from Coastal Development Permit and make the determination\n  that the project is not within the 300-foot appealable zone; and\n  3. Approve the Site Plan as proposed with all applicable entitlements incorporated, subject to\n  the standard list of conditions (attached as Exhibit A to Attachment 1).\nPage 4 of 12\n\f  4. Find the application allowed under the 2023 Growth Policy.\n\nOption: The Commission may impose additional conditions as are deemed necessary at the time\nof discussion to protect the public health, safety and general welfare, including any pertinent\nfactors affecting the establishment, operation or maintenance of the requested use.\n\nAttachments:\n\n  1. Resolution 23-03\n\nPage 5 of 12\n\f  A RESOLUTION OF THE PLANNING COMMISSION OF THE CITY OF\n  AVALON, CALIFORNIA, GRANTING AND APPROVING A SITE PLAN SP 23-\n  01 FOR THE REMODEL OF AN EXISTING RESIDENCE AT 345\n  EUCALYPTUS AVENUE; APN 7480-033-017;\n\n  WHEREAS, Fineline (“Applicant”) filed a Site Plan application (“Application”) for the\nremodel of a two-story single family dwelling located at (“Site”); 345 Eucalyptus Avenue, Avalon,\nCalifornia; further described as assessor’s parcel number 7480-033-017, in the High Density\nResidential (“Flats”) Zone; and\n\n  WHEREAS, This Application for the addition of a bedroom via conversion of existing\ninterior space - resulting in a four bedroom structure was executed in accordance with Avalon\nMunicipal Code (“AMC”) Section 9-8.202; and\n\n  WHEREAS, on January 18, 2023 the Planning Commission of the City of Avalon (“Planning\nCommission”) considering the Application at a regularly scheduled meeting at which time all\npersons wishing to testify in connection with the Application were heard and the Application was\ncomprehensively reviewed; and\n\n  WHEREAS, pursuant to Title 14, California Code of Regulations, section 15367, the City of\nAvalon is the lead agency for purposes of the Project; and\n\n  WHEREAS, the proposed remodel will not have any growth inducing effect and does not\ncreate the need for additional employees, thus the project is allowed by the 2023 Growth Policy;\nand\n\n  WHEREAS, based on the entire record before the Planning Commission and all written\nand oral evidence presented to the Planning Commission, the Planning Commission hereby finds\nthat the Project is categorically exempt under the following provisions:\n\nSection 15301(a) of the State CEQA Guidelines – Existing Facilities.\n\n  (a) Interior or exterior alterations involving such things as interior partitions, plumbing, and\n  electrical conveyances;\n\n  WHEREAS, the project is located in the Coastal Zone, but outside of the 300 feet\nappealable seaward area boundary and is exempt from a Coastal Development Permit in\naccordance with Municipal Code section 9-8.102(b)(1) ; and\n\n  WHEREAS, all other legal prerequisites for the consideration of the Site Plan approval\nhave occurred.\n\n  NOW, THEREFORE, the Planning Commission of the City of Avalon does hereby determine\nand find as follows:\n\n  Section 1. The project is Categorically Exempt from the State CEQA Guidelines pursuant\n\nPage 6 of 12\n\fto Section 15301, Class 1 - “Existing Facilities” subsection (a)\n\n  Section 2. All Recitals shall be incorporated into this Resolution by reference. Pursuant to\nSection AMC 9-8.203, and based on the entire record before the Planning Commission, all written\nand oral evidence presented to the Planning Commission, and the findings set forth below, the\nPlanning Commission approves the Site Plan subject to the conditions set forth in the attached\nExhibit “A”\n\n  (a) Every use, development of land and application of development standards shall take\n  place in compliance with all applicable provisions of this title.\n\n  The proposed project does not have any foreseeable effect on the use or development\n  standards associated with this property. As the intensity of residential use is measured\n  by dwelling units/acre, and the existing density (1 unit – SFR) is not changing, there\n  should be no change in intensity. Bedroom count is not a contributing factor to this\n  calculation.\n\n  (b) Every use, development of land and application of development standards shall be\n  considered on the basis of the suitability of the site for the particular use or\n  development in-tended, and the total development, including the application of\n  prescribed development standards, shall be so arranged as to avoid traffic\n  congestion, insure the protection of public health, safety and general welfare, prevent\n  adverse effects on neighboring property and shall be in general accord with all\n  elements of the General Plan.\n\n  As discussed, the scope of work is limited to internal reconfiguration within an existing\n  structure, which should not have an effect on neighboring properties or its accordance\n  with the General Plan. The applicant will need to follow standard building permit\n  requirements and meet all applicable codes.\n\n  (c) Every use, development of land, application of development standards shall be\n  considered on the basis of suitable and functional development design, but is not\n  intended that such approval be interpreted to require a particular style or type of\n  architecture.\n\n  The design of the proposed remodel is suitable for a single family residential structure\n  and will have minimal effect on the architectural style of the building. The addition of\n  one bedroom via conversion of existing interior space reflects the original intent of the\n  design, a two story, four bedroom single family dwelling totaling 1338 square feet. No\n  changes to the number of bathrooms, number of stories, or use as a single family\n  residence would occur.\n\n  Section 3. Pursuant to section 9-8.102 of the Code, the Planning Commission hereby finds\nthe project exempt from a Coastal Development Permit.\n\nPage 7 of 12\n\f  Section 4. Based on the entire record before the Planning Commission and all written and\noral evidence presented to the Planning Commission, the Planning Commission hereby finds the\nnature and extent of the dedications, reservations, impact fees, and other exactions set forth in\nExhibit “A”, if any, are reasonably related to public needs and roughly proportional to the impacts\ncreated by the entitlements for the Site.\n\n  Section 5. Pursuant to Section 9-8.204 of the Code, the site plan shall become effective\nfifteen (15) days after the receipt by the Applicant of a written notice of action taken unless an\nappeal has been made to the City Clerk of Avalon within the said fifteen (15) days.\n\n  Section 6. The Chair of the Planning Commission shall sign this Resolution and the\nSecretary of the Planning Commission shall certify the adoption thereof.\n\n  NOW, THEREFORE BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED by the Planning Commission of the City of\nAvalon that the Application is hereby approved, subject to the Conditions of Approval in Exhibit\nA, and that a copy of this Resolution be delivered to the City Clerk as well as the Applicant.\n\n  PASSED, APPROVED AND ADOPTED this 18th day of January, 2023.\n\n  ________________________\n  Chairman Bart Glass\n\nATTEST:\n\n___________________________\nDave Maistros, City Manager\n\nCERTIFICATE\n\n  I, Donna Terpinitz-Romo, hereby certify that the foregoing Resolution 23-03 was\nadopted by the Planning Commission of the City of Avalon at a regular meeting held on the\n18th day of January 2023, by the following vote, to wit:\n\nAYES:\nNOES:\nABSENT:\nABSTAIN:\n\n  __________________________________________\n  Donna Terpinitz-Romo Administrative Assistant\n\nPage 8 of 12\n\f  Exhibit A\n\n  Conditions of Approval\n  Street Address: 345 Eucalyptus Ave\n  PC# 2830\n\n  The following conditions are specific to PC Application #2830:\n\n  1. This project approval shall expire on January 18, 2026 unless a time extension is requested,\n  approved, and filed with the Planning Department prior to expiration.\n\n  2. The Commission may impose additional conditions as are deemed necessary at the time of\n  discussion to protect the public health, safety and general welfare, including any pertinent\n  factors affecting the establishment, operation or maintenance of the requested use.\n\n  The following conditions require additional action by or documentation from, the applicant\n  before building permits can be issued:\n\n  The California Department of Fish & Wildlife filing fee shall be submitted as required by\n  California Environmental Quality Act (CEQA) statute, Section 21089(b) and Fish and Game\n  Code Section 711.4. The fee should be paid within five (5) days of approval of the environmental\n  filing at the Los Angeles County Clerk’s Office. Once fees have been paid, the applicant shall\n  submit a copy of all documentation to the City of Avalon, verifying the fees have been paid.\n  Said permit shall not become valid, vested or operative until the fee has been paid.\n\n  No development, including demolition/construction work, shall commence on the subject\n  property until appropriate permits have been applied for and obtained from City of Avalon\n  Building  and  Safety  Department.\n\n  If applicable, confirmation is provided by SCE that this project will not require additional fresh\n  water.\n\n  Projects resulting in demolition of existing structures or projects exceeding $75,000 in value\n  must provide certification of the condition of the sewer lateral before building permits can be\n  issued.\n\n  Projects involving structures constructed before 1979 and that requires any demolition, must be\n  assessed for the presence of asbestos, lead and other hazardous materials which shall be handled\n  in accordance with applicable State codes, South Coast Air Quality Management District\n  regulations, as well as the Federal National Emissions Standard for Hazardous Air Pollutants\n  (NESHAP). Copies of inspection results and shipping manifests (when disposal is required)\n  must be provided prior to issuance of a demolition permit or building (major renovation) permit.\n\n  Projects requiring excavation and/or grading in a mapped archeologically area defined in the\n  Cultural Resources Management Plan for the City, may be required to have a qualified\n  archeological monitor onsite during excavation and/or grading.\n\n  Projects requiring obstruction of the public right of way must obtain obstruction permits prior to\n  or simultaneous with the issuance of a building permit.\nPage 9 of 12\n\f  Projects requiring excavation in the public right of way must obtain excavation permits prior to\n  or simultaneous with the issuance of a building permit.\n\n  Projects subject to payment of Long Beach Unified School District fees must provide proof of\n  payment or exemption prior to issuance of the building permit.\n\n  The following conditions apply to all projects:\n\n  The project shall be under construction (defined as foundations in and framing in progress)\n  within 36 months of this approval or all permits will be null and void.\n\n  Projects located in the floodplain as defined by the FIRM (Flood Insurance Rate Map), must\n  provide a base flood elevation certificate for the new or substantially improved structure\n  resulting from this application before a building permit can be issued.\n\n  Legal lot status must be verified in compliance with local and state subdivision laws, including\n  the Subdivision Map Act (“SMA”) prior to the expiration of this project approval. Should any\n  land division violation be determined, the applicant may request a Certificate of Compliance to\n  create a buildable parcel.\n\n  If the City discovers that material facts were not disclosed by applicant or that facts provided by\n  the applicant were different than represented, the City may impose additional conditions of\n  approval to mitigate any adverse impacts. The Planning Commission may revoke or modify the\n  approval in the future if the Commission finds that the use to which the permit allows is\n  detrimental to health, safety, comfort, general welfare of the public; constitutes a public\n  nuisance; if the permit was obtained or is being used by fraud; and/or if one or more the\n  conditions upon which a permit was granted are in noncompliance or have been violated.\n\n  The project shall be developed and maintained in strict accordance with the approved plans\n  unless written approval is given by the Planning Department.\n\n  This approval does not abridge or supersede the regulatory powers and permit requirements of\n  any federal, state, or local agency requirements, which may retain a regulatory or advisory\n  function as specified by statute or ordinance. Authorization by the Planning Department does\n  not waive the requirement that the project comply with all sections of the Avalon Municipal\n  code and all other relevant local/state/federal regulations. The applicant shall obtain and\n  maintain permits as may be required from each agency.\n\n  The applicant is responsible for ensuring that all project workers are informed of, understand,\n  and agree to abide by the approved plans and project conditions.\n\n  All construction on the project shall be limited to Monday through Saturday, 8am to 7pm. No\n  construction shall occur on Sundays.\n\n  All project contractors and sub-contractors must maintain a City of Avalon business licenses in\n  compliance with all local, state, and federal laws allowing them to work in the City.\n\nPage 10 of 12\n\f  Non-combustible roofing materials shall be used subject to approval by the Building and Fire\n  Departments.\n\n  Approved fire walls shall be constructed as required by the adopted Building Code.\n\n  Fire Suppression system is required if the project is for:\n  a) New Structure; or\n  b) Rehabilitation, remodeling or reconstruction of twenty five percent (25%) or more of the\n  gross square footage of an existing commercial or multiple unit residential building or\n  structure; or\n  c) Rehabilitation, remodeling or reconstruction of fifty percent (50%) or more of the gross\n  square footage of a single unit residential building or structure and the cost of the work\n  is more than $10,000.00.\n  d) Change in the use of the building or structure. Change in use is defined as “those\n  alterations which significantly alter the use, increase the number of dwelling units in\n  an existing building or result in a building being held open to the public where the prior\n  use was for private purposes only. This could include changes from principally\n  permitted to conditionally permitted uses, but excludes the conversion of a single\n  family residence from transient to non-transient rental or from non-transient to transient\n  rental. The requirement shall apply to changes from commercial to residential, or\n  residential to commercial, but would not include expansions of existing commercial\n  uses, such as the addition of table service at an existing coffee bar or the addition of\n  service of liquor at a restaurant.”\n  e) Work has been done without City permit(s) and City files do not contain information\n  to enable us to determine extent of changes to building. In order to avoid an order to\n  install a fire sprinkler system, you must provide valid documentation of the structural\n  plan as it existed prior to the un-permitted work.\n\n  The site shall be kept in a neat manner at all times and the landscaping shall be continuously\n  maintained in a healthy and thriving condition. During construction activities, the applicant shall\n  remove daily accumulation of mud and dirt from any roads adjacent to the site; any removal of\n  vegetation or construction materials shall be lawfully disposed of.\n\n  Non-potable water must be used for dust control during construction.\n  The entire sanitation system shall be connected to the City salt water system.\n\n  The design and construction of all sewers shall be approved by the Department of Public Works,\n  including the design and construction of sewer connections and laterals.\n\n  Structural drainage shall be provided by use of roof gutters and downspouts which allow for\n  proper drainage away from the foundation of any structure and which do not cause any pooling\n  of standing water.\n\n  All plans and specifications must be approved by the City of Avalon Building Department prior\n  to construction beginning.\n\n  City staff shall review and approve location, size and noise output of any mechanical devices.\n\nPage 11 of 12\n\f  Any changes mandated by the City of Avalon Building Department that would require an\n  amended site plan must be reviewed and approved by the Planning Commission before any\n  construction takes place.\n\n  Owner/Applicant must offer to dedicate to the City the following easements measuring at least\n  10 feet in width, if applicable:\n\n  Public utilities easement\n  Water line easement\n  Sewer facilities easement\n  Landscape easement\n  Storm drain easement\n\n  Any existing public improvements which are damaged as a result of construction regarding the\n  project shall be repaired or replaced to the satisfaction of City staff at the Owner/Applicant’s\n  sole cost before a certificate of occupancy can be issued.\n\n  Owner/Applicant agrees to indemnify, defend, and hold the City harmless, with counsel\n  reasonably acceptable to the City, from costs and expenses, including attorney fees, incurred by\n  the City or held to be the liability of the City in connection with the City’s defense of its actions\n  in any proceeding brought in any State or Federal court challenging the City’s actions with\n  respect to the project. Owner/Applicant understands and acknowledges that the City is under no\n  obligation to defend any legal actions challenging the project.\n\n  Any preexisting Municipal Code violations at the property may be required, in City’s sole\n  discretion, to be resolved prior to issuance of a certificate of occupancy, even if such Code\n  violations are unrelated to the project.\n\n  Project must comply with all mitigation measures noted in any related environmental\n  assessments performed in connection with this project, prior to receiving a certificate of\n  occupancy.\n\n  All conditions of approval must be completed to the satisfaction of the City Planning Department\n  prior to occupancy of the project.\n\n  Project applicant must pay the City all statutory fees under the City’s fee ordinance, unless\n  otherwise waived by the Director.\n\nPage 12 of 12\n\f  AVALON PLANNING COMMISSION\n  STAFF REPORT – PC# 2831\n  Wednesday, January 18, 2023\n\n  Application File: Variance, Site Plan\n  Submitted by: Susanna Gutierrez Assistant Planner\nItem No.  – Public Hearing\n\nProject Description: An application for the modification of a front Setback Variance and Site\nPlan for improvements to an existing single family dwelling in the HDR (flats) zone. The project\nis in the Coastal Zone but exempt from a Coastal Development Permit, as well as CEQA per\nSection 15301 - Existing facilities. The Planning Commission will determine whether the project\nis within the 300-foot appealable seaward area boundary.\n\n  Owner/Applicant:  Straight Up Builders\n  Location:  327 Eucalyptus Ave\n  APN:  7480-033-009\n  PC#:  2831\n  Zone:  High Density Residential (HDR - Flats)\n  File Date:  12-13-2022\n\nPage 1 of 16\n\f  BACKGROUND\n\nHigh Density Residential development standards associated with this neighborhood require a\nminimum front yard of three feet. The applicant was previously granted a Variance in order to\nexceed the setback requirement enclosing an existing front porch. With this application, the\nproperty owner proposes to extend the existing Variance to accommodate the installation of a new\nbay window. The only change to the front yard configuration posed by this project is the addition\nof a 28SF bay window, on the first floor almost directly abutting the property line.\n\n  GENERAL PLAN, LAND USE, AND ZONING COMPLIANCE\n\nSite Plan:\nA site plan approval is required since the project doesn’t qualify as a “minor renovation” per\nmunicipal code section 9-3.613(a).\n\n  “(a) The project only requires approval of a site plan and does not require a variance,\n  conditional use permit, coastal development permit, zoning or general plan amendment,\n  or other permit, entitlement, or approval necessitating Planning Commission or City\n  Council review;”\n\nFurthermore, AMC Section 9-8.203 requires that the Site Plan be approved based upon three (3)\nfactors and principles. These criteria and the corresponding explanation of project compliance are\nas follows:\n\n(a) Every use, development of land and application of development standards shall take place\n  in compliance with all applicable provisions of this title. The proposed project does not have\n  any foreseeable effect on the use or development standards associated with this property with\n  the exception of the setback variance as discussed in this report. As the intensity of residential\n  use is measured by dwelling units/acre, and the existing density (1 unit – SFR) is not changing,\n  there should be no change in intensity.\n\n(b) Every use, development of land and application of development standards shall be\n  considered on the basis of the suitability of the site for the particular use or development in-\n  tended, and the total development, including the application of prescribed development\n  standards, shall be so arranged as to avoid traffic congestion, insure the protection of public\n  health, safety and general welfare, prevent adverse effects on neighboring property and shall\n  be in general accord with all elements of the General Plan. As discussed, the scope of work\n  will have no impact on the public right of way or vehicular sight clearances. This application\n  only pertains to the first story; the upper floor’s impact on the sight corridor is to remain\n  unchanged. All other development standards as outlined in the AMC will be maintained. The\n  scope of work will not have a substantial effect on neighboring properties or the site’s\n  accordance with the General Plan because it is a minor addition of a bay window that is\n  harmonious with existing development. The applicant will need to follow standard building\n  permit requirements and meet all applicable codes.\n\nPage 2 of 16\n\f(c) Every use, development of land, application of development standards shall be considered\n  on the basis of suitable and functional development design, but is not intended that such\n  approval be interpreted to require a particular style or type of architecture. The design of the\n  proposed remodel is suitable for a single family residential structure and will have minimal\n  effect on the architectural style of the building. No changes to the number of bathrooms,\n  number of stories, or use as a single family residence would occur.\n\nVariance:\n\nHigh Density Residential development standards associated with this neighborhood require a\nminimum front yard of three feet. The applicant was previously granted a Variance in order to\nexceed the setback requirement enclosing an existing front porch. The only change to the front\nyard configuration posed by this project is the addition of a 28SF bay window, on the first floor\nalmost directly abutting the property line.\n\nStaff is recommending the approval of the requested Variance application, which requires the\nPlanning Commission make the following findings as set forth in AMC section 9-8.405:\n\n  (a) The strict application of the zoning ordinance will result in unnecessary hardship as\n  follows:\n\n  The strict application of the zoning ordinance does not take into account the unique\n  characteristics of this pedestrian only residential street, which is characterized by the\n  communal quality provided by the otherwise underutilized space. As such, the project is\n  consistent with the existing residential development. Furthermore, the bay window\n\nPage 3 of 16\n\f  addition would maintain visibility as well as provide natural lighting, along with other\n  green energy benefits to the parklet-esque area.\n\n  (b) There are exceptional circumstances that apply to development of said property which\n  do not apply to other property in the neighborhood.\n\n  Eucalyptus Avenue stands out as exceptional among the almost identical grid development\n  of the “flats” as exceptional for being gated and closed off from vehicular traffic. As such,\n  the main mode of transport in this area is pedestrian. In addition, the current setback\n  configuration was authorized via Variance previously, which will only be changing a total\n  of 28 square feet and span approximately ¼ of the lot frontage.\n\n  (c) The granting of this Variance will not be detrimental to the public welfare or\n  neighborhood property because:\n\n  •  It will have no impact on the public right of way or vehicular sight clearances.\n  •  This application only pertains to the first story; the upper floor’s impact on the sight\n  corridor is to remain unchanged\n  •  All other development standards as outlined in the AMC will be maintained.\n\n  (d) Granting of this Variance will not be contrary to the objectives of the Local Coastal\n  Program as adopted because:\n\n  The objective of the LCP is to protect coastal resources; the granting of this variance\n  application will have no visual or physical impact on coastal access. Property\n  improvements associated with minor remodels like this minimize blight and promote\n  building code compliance, having a positive effect on overall livability of the town and\n  maintaining coastal access for both residents and visitors. Furthermore, it is Staff’s\n  recommendation that the project be found exempt from a Coastal Development Permit, as\n  well as outside of the 300-foot appealable seaward boundary delineating Coastal\n  Commission jurisdiction.\n\nAnnual Growth Policy:\nStaff recommends that the Planning Commission make the finding as required by the 2023\nGrowth Policy that the proposed remodel will not have any growth inducing effect and does not\ncreate the need for additional employees, thus the project is allowed by the 2023 Growth Policy.\nAs residential use intensity is measured by dwelling units/acre, and the existing density is not\nchanging, there should be no change in intensity. The growth policy provides that new\ndevelopment will only be permitted for the following classes of projects and almost all require\nconfirmation from Southern California Edison Company (“SCE”) that adequate fresh water is\navailable:\n  1. The repair, in-kind replacement, remodeling or enlargement of existing commercial\n  and residential structures, subject to specified conditions;\n\n  ENVIRONMENTAL DETERMINATION\nPage 4 of 16\n\fCEQA:\nThe project is Categorically Exempt under Section 15301(e)(1)of the State CEQA Guidelines\nExisting facilities which states the following:\n  - 15301(e) Additions to existing structures provided that the addition will not result in an\n  increase of more than:\n  (1) 50 percent of the floor area of the structures before the addition, or 2,500\n  square feet, whichever is less;\n\nCoastal Development:\n  All projects within the City of Avalon are located\n  in the Coastal Zone. Under AMC 9-8.103(h), the\n  Planning Commission must determine whether the\n  project is within the 300 foot appealable seaward\n  area boundary. Based on the appealable area map\n  excerpt included, it is staff’s recommendation that\n  the Planning Commission make the finding that the\n  project is not within the 300 foot appealable zone.\n  City staff determined the project to be exempt from\n  a Coastal Development Permit according to\n  Municipal Code section 9-8.102(b)(1), as an\n  “improvement to an existing single-family\n  residence” that does not fall into any of the\n  subcategories “involving a risk of adverse\n  environment effect.”\n\nFresh Water Availability (SCE): Staff has determined the scope of work exempt from requiring\na Fresh Water Allocation based on the zero net change in bedrooms.\n\nRecommendations: Based on the findings, Staff recommends the Planning Commission:\n\n  1. Find the project categorically exempt under CEQA § 15301, Class 1 – Existing Facilities.\n  2. Find the application exempt from Coastal Development Permit and make the determination\n  that the project is not within the 300-foot appealable zone; and\n  3. Approve the Site Plan as proposed with all applicable entitlements incorporated, subject to\n  the standard list of conditions (attached as Exhibit A to Attachment 1).\n  4. Adopt the findings of modification to the front Setback Variance.\n  5. Find the application allowed under the 2023 Growth Policy.\n  6. Adopt Resolution 23-03.\n\nOption: The Commission may impose additional conditions as are deemed necessary at the time\nof discussion to protect the public health, safety and general welfare, including any pertinent\nfactors affecting the establishment, operation or maintenance of the requested use.\n\nPage 5 of 16\n\fPage 6 of 16\n\fAttachments:\n\n  1. Resolution 23-03\n\n  RESOLUTION OF THE PLANNING COMMISSION OF THE CITY OF\n  AVALON, CALIFORNIA, GRANTING A CEQA EXEMPTION AND COASTAL\n  DEVELOPMENT PERMIT EXEMPTION AND APPROVING A SITE PLAN SP\n  23-02 AND VARAINCE AMENDMENT 23-01 FOR THE REMODEL OF AN\n  EXISTING RESIDENCE AT 327 EUCALYPTUS AVENUE; APN 7480-033-009;\n\n  WHEREAS, Straight Up Builders (“Applicant”) filed a Site Plan application (“Application”)\nfor improvements to an existing single family dwelling at (“Site”); 327 Eucalyptus Avenue,\nAvalon, California; further described as assessor’s parcel number 7480-033-009, in the High\nDensity Residential (“Flats”) Zone; and\n\n  WHEREAS, This Application for the modification of a front Setback Variance and Site Plan\nfor improvements was executed in accordance with Avalon Municipal Code (“AMC”) Section 9-\n8.613; and\n\n  WHEREAS, on January 18, 2023 the Planning Commission of the City of Avalon (“Planning\nCommission”) considering the Application at a regularly scheduled meeting at which time all\npersons wishing to testify in connection with the Application were heard and the Application was\ncomprehensively reviewed; and\n\n  WHEREAS, pursuant to Title 14, California Code of Regulations, section 15367, the City of\nAvalon is the lead agency for purposes of the Project; and\n\n  WHEREAS, the proposed remodel will not have any growth inducing effect and does not\ncreate the need for additional employees, thus the project is allowed by the 2023 Growth Policy;\nand\n\n  WHEREAS, based on the entire record before the Planning Commission and all written\nand oral evidence presented to the Planning Commission, the Planning Commission hereby finds\nthat the Project is categorically exempt under the following provisions:\n\nSection 15301(e)(1) of the State CEQA Guidelines – Existing Facilities.\n\n  -  (e) Additions to existing structures provided that the addition will not result in an increase\n  of more than:\n  (1) 50 percent of the floor area of the structures before the addition, or 2,500\n  square feet, whichever is less;\n\n  WHEREAS, the project is located in the Coastal Zone, but outside of the 300 feet\nappealable seaward area boundary and is exempt from a Coastal Development Permit in\naccordance with Municipal Code section 9-8.102(b)(1) ; and\n\nPage 7 of 16\n\f  WHEREAS, all other legal prerequisites for the consideration of the Site Plan approval\nhave occurred.\n\n  NOW, THEREFORE, the Planning Commission of the City of Avalon does hereby determine\nand find as follows:\n\n  Section 1. The project is Categorically Exempt from the State CEQA Guidelines pursuant\nto Section 15301, Class 1 - “Existing Facilities” subsection (e) (1)\n\n  Section 2. All Recitals shall be incorporated into this Resolution by reference.\n\n  Section 3. Pursuant to Section AMC 9-8.203, and based on the entire record before the\nPlanning Commission, all written and oral evidence presented to the Planning Commission, and\nthe findings set forth below, the Planning Commission approves the Site Plan subject to the\nconditions set forth in the attached Exhibit “A.”\n\n  (a) Every use, development of land and application of development standards shall take\n  place in compliance with all applicable provisions of this title.\n\n  The proposed project does not have any foreseeable effect on the use or development\n  standards associated with this property. As the intensity of residential use is measured\n  by dwelling units/acre, and the existing density (1 unit – SFR) is not changing, there\n  should be no change in intensity.\n\n  (b) Every use, development of land and application of development standards shall be\n  considered on the basis of the suitability of the site for the particular use or\n  development in-tended, and the total development, including the application of\n  prescribed development standards, shall be so arranged as to avoid traffic\n  congestion, insure the protection of public health, safety and general welfare, prevent\n  adverse effects on neighboring property and shall be in general accord with all\n  elements of the General Plan.\n\n  As discussed, the scope of work will have no impact on the public right of way or\n  vehicular sight clearances. This application only pertains to the first story; the upper\n  floor’s impact on the sight corridor is to remain unchanged. All other development\n  standards as outlined in the AMC will be maintained. The scope of work will not have\n  a substantial effect on neighboring properties or the site’s accordance with the General\n  Plan because it is a minor addition of a bay window that is harmonious with existing\n  development. The applicant will need to follow standard building permit requirements\n  and meet all applicable codes.\n\n  (c) Every use, development of land, application of development standards shall be\n  considered on the basis of suitable and functional development design, but is not\n  intended that such approval be interpreted to require a particular style or type of\n  architecture.\nPage 8 of 16\n\f  The design of the proposed remodel is suitable for a single family residential structure\n  and will have minimal effect on the architectural style of the building. The addition of\n  the addition of a 28SF bay window will. No changes to the number of bathrooms,\n  number of stories, or use as a single family residence would occur.\n\n  Section 4. Pursuant to Section AMC 9-8.405, and based on the entire record before the\nPlanning Commission, all written and oral evidence presented to the Planning Commission, and\nthe findings set forth below, the Planning Commission approves the Variance subject to the\nconditions set forth in the attached Exhibit “A.”\n\n  (a) The strict application of the zoning ordinance will result in unnecessary hardship as\n  follows:\n\n  The strict application of the zoning ordinance does not take into account the unique\n  characteristics of this pedestrian only residential street, which is characterized by the\n  communal quality provided by the otherwise underutilized space. As such, the project is\n  consistent with the existing residential development. Furthermore, the bay window\n  addition would maintain visibility as well as provide natural lighting, along with other\n  green energy benefits to the parklet-esque area.\n\n  (b) There are exceptional circumstances that apply to development of said property which\n  do not apply to other property in the neighborhood.\n\n  Eucalyptus Avenue stands out as exceptional among the almost identical grid development\n  of the “flats” as exceptional for being gated and closed off from vehicular traffic. As such,\n  the main mode of transport in this area is pedestrian. In addition, the current setback\n  configuration was authorized via Variance previously, which will only be changing a total\n  of 28 square feet and span approximately ¼ of the lot frontage.\n\n  (c) The granting of this Variance will not be detrimental to the public welfare or\n  neighborhood property because:\n\n  It will have no impact on the public right of way or vehicular sight clearances. This\n  application only pertains to the first story; the upper floor’s impact on the sight corridor is\n  to remain unchanged. All other development standards as outlined in the AMC will be\n  maintained.\n\n  (d) Granting of this Variance will not be contrary to the objectives of the Local Coastal\n  Program as adopted because:\n\n  The objective of the LCP is to protect coastal resources; the granting of this variance\n  application will have no visual or physical impact on coastal access. Property\n  improvements associated with minor remodels like this minimize blight and promote\n  building code compliance, having a positive effect on overall livability of the town and\n  maintaining coastal access for both residents and visitors. Furthermore, it is Staff’s\n\nPage 9 of 16\n\f  recommendation that the project be found exempt from a Coastal Development Permit, as\n  well as outside of the 300-foot appealable seaward boundary delineating Coastal\n  Commission jurisdiction.\n\n  Section 5. Pursuant to section 9-8.102 of the Code, the Planning Commission hereby finds\nthe project exempt from a Coastal Development Permit.\n\n  Section 6. Based on the entire record before the Planning Commission and all written and\noral evidence presented to the Planning Commission, the Planning Commission hereby finds the\nnature and extent of the dedications, reservations, impact fees, and other exactions set forth in\nExhibit “A”, if any, are reasonably related to public needs and roughly proportional to the impacts\ncreated by the entitlements for the Site.\n\n  Section 7. Pursuant to Section 9-8.204 of the Code, the site plan shall become effective\nfifteen (15) days after the receipt by the Applicant of a written notice of action taken unless an\nappeal has been made to the City Clerk of Avalon within the said fifteen (15) days.\n\n  Section 8. The Chair of the Planning Commission shall sign this Resolution and the\nSecretary of the Planning Commission shall certify the adoption thereof.\n\n  NOW, THEREFORE BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED by the Planning Commission of the City of\nAvalon that the Application is hereby approved, subject to the Conditions of Approval in Exhibit\nA, and that a copy of this Resolution be delivered to the City Clerk as well as the Applicant.\n\n  PASSED, APPROVED AND ADOPTED this 18th day of January, 2023.\n\n  ________________________\n  Chairman Bart Glass\n\nATTEST:\n\n___________________________\nDave Maistros, City Manager\n\nCERTIFICATE\n\n  I, Donna Terpinitz-Romo, hereby certify that the foregoing Resolution 23-03 was\nadopted by the Planning Commission of the City of Avalon at a regular meeting held on the\n18th day of January 2023, by the following vote, to wit:\n\nPage 10 of 16\n\fAYES:\nNOES:\nABSENT:\nABSTAIN:\n\n  __________________________________________\n  Donna Terpinitz-Romo Senior Administrative Assistant\n\nPage 11 of 16\n\f  Exhibit A\n\n  Conditions of Approval\n  Street Address: 327 Eucalyptus Ave\n  PC# 2831\n\n  The following conditions are specific to PC Application #2831:\n\n  1. This project approval shall expire on January 18, 2026 unless a time extension is requested,\n  approved, and filed with the Planning Department prior to expiration.\n\n  2. The Commission may impose additional conditions as are deemed necessary at the time of\n  discussion to protect the public health, safety and general welfare, including any pertinent\n  factors affecting the establishment, operation or maintenance of the requested use.\n\n  The following conditions require additional action by or documentation from, the applicant\n  before building permits can be issued:\n\n  The California Department of Fish & Wildlife filing fee shall be submitted as required by\n  California Environmental Quality Act (CEQA) statute, Section 21089(b) and Fish and Game\n  Code Section 711.4. The fee should be paid within five (5) days of approval of the environmental\n  filing at the Los Angeles County Clerk’s Office. Once fees have been paid, the applicant shall\n  submit a copy of all documentation to the City of Avalon, verifying the fees have been paid.\n  Said permit shall not become valid, vested or operative until the fee has been paid.\n\n  No development, including demolition/construction work, shall commence on the subject\n  property until appropriate permits have been applied for and obtained from City of Avalon\n  Building and Safety Department.\n\n  If applicable, confirmation is provided by SCE that this project will not require additional fresh\n  water.\n\n  Projects resulting in demolition of existing structures or projects exceeding $75,000 in value\n  must provide certification of the condition of the sewer lateral before building permits can be\n  issued.\n\n  Projects involving structures constructed before 1979 and that requires any demolition, must be\n  assessed for the presence of asbestos, lead and other hazardous materials which shall be handled\n  in accordance with applicable State codes, South Coast Air Quality Management District\n  regulations, as well as the Federal National Emissions Standard for Hazardous Air Pollutants\n  (NESHAP). Copies of inspection results and shipping manifests (when disposal is required)\n  must be provided prior to issuance of a demolition permit or building (major renovation) permit.\n\n  Projects requiring excavation and/or grading in a mapped archeologically area defined in the\n  Cultural Resources Management Plan for the City, may be required to have a qualified\n  archeological monitor onsite during excavation and/or grading.\n\n  Projects requiring obstruction of the public right of way must obtain obstruction permits prior to\nPage 12 of 16\n\f  or simultaneous with the issuance of a building permit.\n\n  Projects requiring excavation in the public right of way must obtain excavation permits prior to\n  or simultaneous with the issuance of a building permit.\n\n  Projects subject to payment of Long Beach Unified School District fees must provide proof of\n  payment or exemption prior to issuance of the building permit.\n\n  The following conditions apply to all projects:\n\n  The project shall be under construction (defined as foundations in and framing in progress)\n  within 36 months of this approval or all permits will be null and void.\n\n  Projects located in the floodplain as defined by the FIRM (Flood Insurance Rate Map), must\n  provide a base flood elevation certificate for the new or substantially improved structure\n  resulting from this application before a building permit can be issued.\n\n  Legal lot status must be verified in compliance with local and state subdivision laws, including\n  the Subdivision Map Act (“SMA”) prior to the expiration of this project approval. Should any\n  land division violation be determined, the applicant may request a Certificate of Compliance to\n  create a buildable parcel.\n\n  If the City discovers that material facts were not disclosed by applicant or that facts provided by\n  the applicant were different than represented, the City may impose additional conditions of\n  approval to mitigate any adverse impacts. The Planning Commission may revoke or modify the\n  approval in the future if the Commission finds that the use to which the permit allows is\n  detrimental to health, safety, comfort, general welfare of the public; constitutes a public\n  nuisance; if the permit was obtained or is being used by fraud; and/or if one or more the\n  conditions upon which a permit was granted are in noncompliance or have been violated.\n\n  The project shall be developed and maintained in strict accordance with the approved plans\n  unless written approval is given by the Planning Department.\n\n  This approval does not abridge or supersede the regulatory powers and permit requirements of\n  any federal, state, or local agency requirements, which may retain a regulatory or advisory\n  function as specified by statute or ordinance. Authorization by the Planning Department does\n  not waive the requirement that the project comply with all sections of the Avalon Municipal\n  code and all other relevant local/state/federal regulations. The applicant shall obtain and\n  maintain permits as may be required from each agency.\n\n  The applicant is responsible for ensuring that all project workers are informed of, understand,\n  and agree to abide by the approved plans and project conditions.\n\n  All construction on the project shall be limited to Monday through Saturday, 8am to 7pm. No\n  construction shall occur on Sundays.\n\n  All project contractors and sub-contractors must maintain a City of Avalon business licenses in\nPage 13 of 16\n\f  compliance with all local, state, and federal laws allowing them to work in the City.\n\n  Non-combustible roofing materials shall be used subject to approval by the Building and Fire\n  Departments.\n\n  Approved fire walls shall be constructed as required by the adopted Building Code.\n\n  Fire Suppression system is required if the project is for:\n  a) New Structure; or\n  b) Rehabilitation, remodeling or reconstruction of twenty five percent (25%) or more of the\n  gross square footage of an existing commercial or multiple unit residential building or\n  structure; or\n  c) Rehabilitation, remodeling or reconstruction of fifty percent (50%) or more of the gross\n  square footage of a single unit residential building or structure and the cost of the work\n  is more than $10,000.00.\n  d) Change in the use of the building or structure. Change in use is defined as “those\n  alterations which significantly alter the use, increase the number of dwelling units in\n  an existing building or result in a building being held open to the public where the prior\n  use was for private purposes only. This could include changes from principally\n  permitted to conditionally permitted uses, but excludes the conversion of a single\n  family residence from transient to non-transient rental or from non-transient to transient\n  rental. The requirement shall apply to changes from commercial to residential, or\n  residential to commercial, but would not include expansions of existing commercial\n  uses, such as the addition of table service at an existing coffee bar or the addition of\n  service of liquor at a restaurant.”\n  e) Work has been done without City permit(s) and City files do not contain information\n  to enable us to determine extent of changes to building. In order to avoid an order to\n  install a fire sprinkler system, you must provide valid documentation of the structural\n  plan as it existed prior to the un-permitted work.\n\n  The site shall be kept in a neat manner at all times and the landscaping shall be continuously\n  maintained in a healthy and thriving condition. During construction activities, the applicant shall\n  remove daily accumulation of mud and dirt from any roads adjacent to the site; any removal of\n  vegetation or construction materials shall be lawfully disposed of.\n\n  Non-potable water must be used for dust control during construction.\n  The entire sanitation system shall be connected to the City salt water system.\n\n  The design and construction of all sewers shall be approved by the Department of Public Works,\n  including the design and construction of sewer connections and laterals.\n\n  Structural drainage shall be provided by use of roof gutters and downspouts which allow for\n  proper drainage away from the foundation of any structure and which do not cause any pooling\n  of standing water.\n\n  All plans and specifications must be approved by the City of Avalon Building Department prior\n\nPage 14 of 16\n\f  to construction beginning.\n\n  City staff shall review and approve location, size and noise output of any mechanical devices.\n\n  Any changes mandated by the City of Avalon Building Department that would require an\n  amended site plan must be reviewed and approved by the Planning Commission before any\n  construction takes place.\n\n  Owner/Applicant must offer to dedicate to the City the following easements measuring at least\n  10 feet in width, if applicable:\n\n  Public utilities easement\n  Water line easement\n  Sewer facilities easement\n  Landscape easement\n  Storm drain easement\n\n  Any existing public improvements which are damaged as a result of construction regarding the\n  project shall be repaired or replaced to the satisfaction of City staff at the Owner/Applicant’s\n  sole cost before a certificate of occupancy can be issued.\n\n  Owner/Applicant agrees to indemnify, defend, and hold the City harmless, with counsel\n  reasonably acceptable to the City, from costs and expenses, including attorney fees, incurred by\n  the City or held to be the liability of the City in connection with the City’s defense of its actions\n  in any proceeding brought in any State or Federal court challenging the City’s actions with\n  respect to the project. Owner/Applicant understands and acknowledges that the City is under no\n  obligation to defend any legal actions challenging the project.\n\n  Any preexisting Municipal Code violations at the property may be required, in City’s sole\n  discretion, to be resolved prior to issuance of a certificate of occupancy, even if such Code\n  violations are unrelated to the project.\n\n  Project must comply with all mitigation measures noted in any related environmental\n  assessments performed in connection with this project, prior to receiving a certificate of\n  occupancy.\n\n  All conditions of approval must be completed to the satisfaction of the City Planning Department\n  prior to occupancy of the project.\n\n  Project applicant must pay the City all statutory fees under the City’s fee ordinance, unless\n  otherwise waived by the Director.\n\nPage 15 of 16\n\fPage 16 of 16\n\f  PLANNING COMMISSION\n  JANUARY 18, 2023\n  STAFF REPORT – PC# 2828\n  RESOLUTION # 23-01\n\nItem No.  – Public Hearing\n\nSubmitted by: Donna Terpinitz-Romo, Senior Administrative Assistant\n\nBACKGROUND\n\nProject Title: Application for a Transient Rental License and Coastal Development Permit\nlocated at 335 Wrigley Rd.\n\nProject Description:\n\nRequest for approval of a Transient Rental License and Coastal Development Permit to allow the\nrenting or leasing of a room or rooms for periods of fewer than thirty (30) consecutive days\nduration located within a 2-story Single Family Dwelling at 335 Wrigley Road. On the first floor of\nthis property are the living areas, dining room, office and 1 bathroom. Located on the 2nd floor\nare four (4) bedrooms and three (3) bathrooms. The project is in the Coastal Zone and subject to\na Coastal Development Permit, which requires a public hearing and Planning Commission\napproval. The Planning Commission will determine whether the project is within the 300-foot\nappealable seaward area boundary. The project is Categorically Exempt under Section 15305 of\nthe State CEQA Guidelines – Minor Alterations in Land Use Limitations.\n\n  Owner/Applicant:  Eric Albert\n  ADDRESS:  335 Wrigley Road\n  APN:  7480-007-044\n  Zone:  Medium Density Residential\n\nPage 1 of 20\n\fLocation Map:\n\nThe Avalon Municipal Code (“AMC”) § 3-1.310(e)(5) provides that the Planning Commission\nshall consider, “whether granting the Transient Rental License shall result in any year round\nresidence located adjacent to the proposed transient rental having more than a majority of the\nadjacent residences permitted or licensed for transient rental use…”. Upon analysis, the\nsaturation map below indicates that less than 50 % of the properties adjacent to 335 Wrigley\nRoad, a two (2) Story Single Family Dwelling, hold either conditional use permits or Transient\nRental licenses.\n\nPage 2 of 20\n\fSaturation Map:\n\nFloor Plan: The property is a two (2) story Single Family Dwelling. The upper level of this unit\nconsists of the living area, Kitchen, office, living room, sitting area and one (1) bathroom the\nlower level of the unit is the sleeping area and consists of four (4) bedrooms and 3 bathrooms.\n\nProperty Manager/24 Hour Contact: The property manager is Catalina Island Vacation Rentals\n(CIVR) Ph., 310-510-2276 and the 24 hour contact is 855-631-5280 Option 9\n\nGROWTH POLICY\n\nSouthern California Edison (SCE) has determined the conversion to a transient rental will not\nrequire a water allocation; thus, the application is in compliance with the 2023 Growth Policy.\n\nPage 3 of 20\n\fCEQA REVIEW\n\nThe proposed project is Categorically Exempt under Section 15305 of the State CEQA\nGuidelines - Minor Alterations in Land Use Limitations (i.e., CUP for transient rental) which do\nnot result in changes in land use or density.\n\nDISCUSSION\n\nThe proposed project involves a request of approval for a transient rental application for a\ntransient rental license under Article 3 of the AMC Transient rental use is permitted in any\nzone upon Planning Commission approval of a Transient Rental License. (AMC 9-6.602(a)) The\nhome is newly purchased and it is the owner’s intent to rent this home short term.\n\nCOASTAL DEVELOPMENT PERMIT:\n\nIn December 2016, the City received an opinion letter from the California Coastal Commission\n(Coastal Commission) noting their position that conversion to transient use constitutes a\nchange in intensity in use and should be authorized pursuant to a coastal development permit.\nBased on the opinion, the application for transient rental use must include a Coastal\nDevelopment Permit (CDP).\n\nThe project is in the Coastal Zone and is subject to a Coastal Development Permit, which\nrequires a public hearing and Planning Commission approval. Specifically, the project requires\na Coastal Development Permit under Section 9-8.102(b)(2)(v) of the AMC, as the proposed\nproject seeks to allow the renting or leasing of a room or rooms with or without table boards\nfor periods of fewer than thirty (30) consecutive days duration (Transient Rental – Section 9-\n6.602 (a) of the AMC) at 335 Wrigley Road.\n\nUnder AMC 9-8.103(h), the Planning Commission must determine whether the project is\nwithin the 300-foot appealable area boundary. Based on the appealable area map excerpt\nincluded below, it is staff’s recommendation that the Planning Commission make the finding\nthat the project is within the 300-foot appealable zone.\n\nAn appeal of the Planning Commission’s Coastal Development Permit action may be filed with\nthe City Council within fifteen (15) days after mailed notice of the action to the applicant. Any\nperson may appeal the Planning Commission’s determination that a proposed project is\nseaward of the appealable seaward area boundary to the Coastal Commission, and following\nexhaustion of local appeals, any aggrieved person may appeal actions on local Coastal\nDevelopment Permits seaward of the appealable area boundary to the Coastal Commission.\n\nPage 4 of 20\n\fThe proposed change in use conforms to the certified Local Coastal Plan (AMC 9-8.103(d) (1))\nbecause it increases access of the public to the coastal zone.\n\nFor the Planning Commission to issue a Coastal Development Permit, the Avalon Municipal\nCode requires that the Planning Commission make certain findings. These findings and\ncorresponding explanations of the project’s compliance are as follows:\n\n(1)  The use is consistent with the goals and objectives of the public access and recreation\npolicies of the California Coastal Act.\n\nThe Coastal Commission opinion letter noted that vacation rentals in the coastal zone is seen\nas providing access to coastal areas and complies with the intent of the California Coastal Act.\n\n(2)  The use is in conformance with the Certified Local Coastal Program for the City of\nAvalon, which includes the Avalon General Plan, the Local Coastal Plan for Avalon, as well as\nthe Avalon Zoning and Affordable Housing Ordinances.\n\nPage 5 of 20\n\fTransient rental use is allowed in any zone with a Transient Rental License, it is supported\n(with appropriate restrictions) by the Coastal Commission and the LCP. The change in use\ncomplies with the Affordable Housing Ordinance because this does not involve an affordable\nhousing project or unit (AMC 9-11.107).\n\n(3)  The use has received all necessary local approvals issuable by the Planning\nCommission, including Growth Ordinance Pre-Approved (in concept) together with water\nallocation, as may be required.\n\nThe change in use is not considered growth and is not subject to the Growth Ordinance and SCE\nhas determined that the change in use does not require an additional water allocation. The\nCommission had determined that limiting occupancy to two (2) per bedroom plus additional\ntwo (2) occupancy during any stage of fresh water restrictions is appropriate. The only local\napproval issuable by the Planning Commission that is applicable to the project is the requested\nCoastal Development Permit and Transient Rental License. There is no saturation analysis\nconcern, as less than a majority of properties adjacent to the proposed transient rental on 335\nWrigley Road presently hold a CUP or Transient Rental License, in accordance with AMC § 3-\n1.310(e)(5).\n\nThe application is allowed under the 2023 Growth Policy.\n\n(4)  The use has been cleared with respect to environmental regulations of CEQA, as\nnecessary.\n\nThe proposed use is categorically exempt under CEQA Section 15305 of the State CEQA\nGuidelines – Minor Alteration in Land Use Limitations (i.e., CUP for transient rental) which\ndoes not result in change in land use and density.\n\n(5)  For all development seaward of the nearest public highway to the shoreline, the\nproposed development conforms to the public access and recreation policies of Chapter 3 of\nthe Coastal Act.\n\nThe project is not located seaward of the nearest public highway to the shoreline, and is\nconsistent with Chapter 3 of the Coastal Act, as stated in the opinion letter referenced above,\nbecause the rental use promotes public access to the coast.\n\nTRANSIENT RENTAL LICENSE\n\nIn order to approve the requested Transient Rental License, the Planning Commission must\nmake three (3) specific findings as required by Section 3-1.310(b) (1-3) of the AMC. These\nfindings and corresponding explanations of the project’s compliance with these findings are as\nfollows:\n\nPage 6 of 20\n\f(1)  The nature, condition, and development of adjacent uses, buildings, and structures\nshall be considered and no proposed transient rental use shall be permitted where such use\nwill adversely affect or be materially detrimental to said adjacent uses, buildings, and\nstructures.\n\nThe subject property and all surrounding properties are developed with single and multi-unit\nresidential units. The requested Transient Rental License does not involve any physical changes\nto the existing residence that would adversely affect or be materially detrimental to adjacent\nuses, buildings, and structures.\n\n(2)  The site for a proposed transient rental use shall be adequate in size and shape to\naccommodate the yards, walls, fences, parking and loading facilities, landscaping, and other\ndevelopment features prescribed in this chapter or as required as a condition in order to\nintegrate said use with the uses in the neighborhood.\n\nThe requested Transient Rental License does not involve any changes to the existing\nimprovements on the subject property, and the transient use has such improvements as to\nintegrate properly in the neighborhood.\n\n(3)  The site on which the transient rental use is located is served by a street that is\nadequate in width and improved as necessary to carry the kind and quantity of traffic that\nsuch use would generate.\n\nThe requested Transient Rental License will allow transient rental use of an existing residence\nthat is served by an improved street adequately designed to serve the property. Under the\nconditions of this approval, no more than 10 persons could live in the property at any given\ntime. The street is of sufficient width to accommodate 10 additional persons relating to this\nuse.\n\nIn addition to the above findings, which apply to all Transient Rental Licenses, Section 3-\n1.310(e)(1-6) of the AMC requires the Planning Commission to consider six (6) additional factors\nprior to taking action on applications for the transient rental license for transient rental\nhousing. The applicable factors and corresponding explanations of how they relate to the\nrequested transient rental license are as follows:\n\n(1) Year-Round Rental Status. In the absence of adequate mitigating measures proposed by\nthe applicant, the application should be denied where the subject property is or has been\nrented for periods of more than thirty (30) consecutive days to the same occupant at any time\nduring the two (2) years preceding the application.\n\nThe Transient Rental Housing Impact Statement submitted by the applicant indicates that the\nproperty has been used as a home by the owner and has not been rented for periods of more\nthan 30 consecutive days to the same occupant at any time in the prior two years.\n\n(2) Neighborhood Impacts. The Planning Commission shall consider the objections of the\n\nPage 7 of 20\n\fowners and occupants of neighboring properties in determining whether the proposed\ntransient rental use is consistent with the surrounding properties and the extent to which such\nimpacts may be mitigated by the imposition of the standard conditions listed below or by the\nimposition of additional conditions. The Planning Commission may consider objections\npreviously made in connection with applications for transient rental use at other properties\nlocated within the same general area as the subject application; however, the Planning\nCommission should consider the time between the prior application and the subject\napplication, the geographic proximity of the subject application to the prior application, any\ndiffering circumstances, and additional mitigating measures proposed by the applicant, and\nchanges in the general area, in determining the weight to be given such prior objections.\n\nThere have been no objections to the requested approval of the transient rental license\napplication. Applicant submitted the Transient Rental License Application on December 15, 2022\nAfter the building official performed Transient rental license inspection on December 15, 2022,\nthe applicant was approved. Sign was posted at this time letting the public know that this unit\nwas applying for transient license.\n\n(3) Single- and Multiple-Unit Properties. In the absence of other negative findings, (a) where\nthe property is the only unit owned by the applicant in the City and the applicant proposes to\nuse the property himself from time to time; or (b) where the subject property contains\nmultiple units, and those other units are utilized for year round rental or occupancy, or the\napplicant owns other separate properties in the City of Avalon which are utilized for year-\nround rental or occupancy, the Planning Commission should approve the application.\n\nThis is the sole property of the owner and the owner does plan on using this property for himself\nand family on occasion.\n\n(4) Condominium Associations Having Ten (10) or More Units. In the case of an application\nconcerning property located within a condominium or similar congregate housing project\nconsisting of ten (10) or more units, the applicant shall be required to demonstrate that any\ngoverning body of the subject association generally allows transient rental uses at the\ncondominium or congregated housing project.\n\nThis provision does not apply to the present transient rental license application because the unit\nis not part of a condominium association having ten (10) or more units.\n\n(5)  The Planning Commission shall consider whether granting the Transient Rental License\nshall result in any year round residence located adjacent to the proposed transient rental\nhaving more than a majority of the adjacent residences permitted or licensed for transient\nrental use. \"Adjacent\" means having a common property line with the proposed transient\nrental unit except for the existence of a public street or alley.\n\nPage 8 of 20\n\fUpon analysis, the saturation map on page three (3) indicates that less than 50% of properties\non 335 Wrigley Road currently hold active CUPs or Transient Rental Licenses. Thus, there will\nnot be a majority of adjacent residences permitted or licensed for transient rental use.\n\n(6) The total square footage of the dwelling unit and the size of each bedroom shall be\nconsidered for determining whether to grant the Transient Rental License.\nThe property is a two (2) story Single Family Dwelling. The upper level of this unit consists of the\nliving area, Kitchen, office, living room, sitting area and one (1) bathroom the lower level of the\nunit is the sleeping area and consists of four (4) bedrooms and 3 bathrooms. The standard\nconditions would in fact allow two (2) additional persons, for a total permitted maximum\noccupancy of ten (10) persons. The home is of adequate size and square footage to allow\ntransient rental use.\n\nRECOMMENDATION\n\nBased on the preceding discussion of the proposed project’s compliance with the applicable\nprinciples and standards of the Avalon Municipal Code, the proposed use will not jeopardize,\nadversely affect, endanger, or otherwise constitute a menace to the public health, safety, or\ngeneral welfare, or be materially detrimental to the property of other persons located in the\nvicinity of such use. Therefore, staff recommends the Planning Commission:\n\n  1. FIND THE PROJECT CATEGORICALLY EXEMPT UNDER SECTION 15305 OF THE STATE\n  CEQA GUIDELINES;\n  2. Adopt the findings necessary to support approval of the Coastal Development Permit,\n  including that the project is within the 300-foot appealable zone;\n  3. Adopt the findings necessary to approve the transient rental license ; and\n  4. Find that the application is allowed under the 2023 Growth Policy.\n  5. Adopt Resolution No. PC 23-01.\n\nATTACHMENTS:\nResolution No. PC 23-01\nFloor Plan\n\nPage 9 of 20\n\f  RESOLUTION NO. 23-01\n\n  A RESOLUTION OF THE PLANNING COMMISSION OF THE CITY OF\n  AVALON, CALIFORNIA, GRANTING AND APPROVING A TRANSIENT\n  RENTAL LICENSE AND COASTAL DEVELOPMENT PERMIT (CDP) AND\n  CEQA EXEMPTION 15305 FOR A PROPERTY LOCATED AT 335 WRIGLEY\n  ROAD AVALON, CALIFORNIA\n\n  WHEREAS, Eric Albert Hugo (“Owner/Applicant”) has applied for a Transient Rental\nLicense and Coastal Development Permit (“CDP”) to allow for the transient rental use in a\nproperty located 335 Wrigley Road in Avalon, California(“Site”);\n\n  WHEREAS, on January 18, 2023 the Planning Commission conducted a duly noticed public\nhearing at which time all persons wishing to testify in connection with the application were heard\nand the application was comprehensively reviewed; and\n\n  WHEREAS, all other legal pre-requisites for the consideration of the Transient Rental\nLicense and CDP have occurred.\n\n  NOW, THEREFORE, the Planning Commission of the City of Avalon does hereby determine\nand find as follows:\n\n  Section 1.  Based on the entire record before the Planning Commission, all written\nand oral evidence presented to the Planning Commission, and the findings set forth in this\nResolution 22-26, the Planning Commission grants the Transient Rental License to allow transient\nrental use on the Site, subject to the conditions of approval set forth in the attached Exhibit “A.”\n\n  Section 2.  Pursuant to 3-1.310(b)(1-3) of the Avalon Municipal Code (“AMC”), and\nbased on the entire record before the Planning Commission and all written and oral evidence\npresented to the Planning Commission, the Planning Commission hereby finds:\n\n(1)  The nature, condition, and development of adjacent uses, buildings, and structures\nshall be considered and no proposed transient rental use shall be permitted where such use\nwill adversely affect or be materially detrimental to said adjacent uses, buildings, and\nstructures.\n\nThe subject property and all surrounding properties are developed with single and multi-unit\nresidential units. The requested Transient Rental License does not involve any physical changes\nto the existing residence that would adversely affect or be materially detrimental to adjacent\nuses, buildings, and structures.\n\n(2)  The site for a proposed transient rental use shall be adequate in size and shape to\naccommodate the yards, walls, fences, parking and loading facilities, landscaping, and other\ndevelopment features prescribed in this chapter or as required as a condition in order to\nintegrate said use with the uses in the neighborhood.\n\nPage 10 of 20\n\fThe requested Transient Rental License does not involve any changes to the existing\nimprovements on the subject property, and the transient use has such improvements as to\nintegrate properly in the neighborhood.\n\n(3)  The site on which the transient rental use is located is served by a street that is\nadequate in width and improved as necessary to carry the kind and quantity of traffic that\nsuch use would generate.\n\nThe requested Transient Rental License will allow transient rental use of an existing residence\nthat is served by an improved street adequately designed to serve the property.\n\nIn addition to the above findings, which apply to all Transient Rental Licenses, Section 3-\n1.310(e)(1-6) of the AMC requires the Planning Commission to consider six (6) additional factors\nprior to taking action on applications for the transient rental license for transient rental\nhousing. The applicable factors and corresponding explanations of how they relate to the\nrequested transient rental license are as follows:\n\n(1) Year-Round Rental Status. In the absence of adequate mitigating measures proposed by\nthe applicant, the application should be denied where the subject property is or has been\nrented for periods of more than thirty (30) consecutive days to the same occupant at any time\nduring the two (2) years preceding the application.\n\nThe Transient Rental Housing Impact Statement submitted by the applicant indicates that the\nproperty has been used as a home by the owner and has not been rented for periods of more\nthan 30 consecutive days to the same occupant at any time in the prior two years.\n\n(2) Neighborhood Impacts. The Planning Commission shall consider the objections of the\nowners and occupants of neighboring properties in determining whether the proposed\ntransient rental use is consistent with the surrounding properties and the extent to which such\nimpacts may be mitigated by the imposition of the standard conditions listed below or by the\nimposition of additional conditions. The Planning Commission may consider objections\npreviously made in connection with applications for transient rental use at other properties\nlocated within the same general area as the subject application; however, the Planning\nCommission should consider the time between the prior application and the subject\napplication, the geographic proximity of the subject application to the prior application, any\ndiffering circumstances, and additional mitigating measures proposed by the applicant, and\nchanges in the general area, in determining the weight to be given such prior objections.\n\nThere have been no objections to the requested approval of the transient rental license\napplication, including by the owners or occupants of any of the neighboring properties.\nApplicant submitted the Transient Rental License Application on 12/15/2022. After Building\nofficial performed Transient rental license inspection on 12/15/2022 the applicant was\napproved. Sign was posted at this time letting the public know that this unit was applying for\ntransient license.\n\nPage 11 of 20\n\f(3) Single- and Multiple-Unit Properties. In the absence of other negative findings, (a) where\nthe property is the only unit owned by the applicant in the City and the applicant proposes to\nuse the property himself from time to time; or (b) where the subject property contains\nmultiple units, and those other units are utilized for year round rental or occupancy, or the\napplicant owns other separate properties in the City of Avalon which are utilized for year-\nround rental or occupancy, the Planning Commission should approve the application.\n\nThis is the sole property of the owner and the owner does plan on using this property for himself\nand family on occasion.\n\n(4) Condominium Associations Having Ten (10) or More Units. In the case of an application\nconcerning property located within a condominium or similar congregate housing project\nconsisting of ten (10) or more units, the applicant shall be required to demonstrate that any\ngoverning body of the subject association generally allows transient rental uses at the\ncondominium or congregated housing project.\n\nThis provision does not apply to the present transient rental license application because the unit\nis not part of a condominium association having ten (10) or more units.\n\n(5)  The Planning Commission shall consider whether granting the Transient Rental License\nshall result in any year round residence located adjacent to the proposed transient rental\nhaving more than a majority of the adjacent residences permitted or licensed for transient\nrental use. \"Adjacent\" means having a common property line with the proposed transient\nrental unit except for the existence of a public street or alley.\n\nUpon analysis, the saturation map on page three (3) indicates that less than 50% of properties\non 335 Wrigley Road currently hold active CUPs or Transient Rental Licenses. Thus, there will\nnot be a majority of adjacent residences permitted or licensed for transient rental use.\n\n(6) The total square footage of the dwelling unit and the size of each bedroom shall be\nconsidered for determining whether to grant the Transient Rental License.\n\nFloor Plan: The property is a two (2) story Single Family Dwelling. The upper level of this unit\nconsists of the living area, Kitchen, office, living room, sitting area and one (1) bathroom the\nlower level of the unit is the sleeping area and consists of four (4) bedrooms and 3 bathrooms.\nThe standard conditions would in fact allow for (2) two additional; persons for a total permitted\noccupancy of (10) persons. The lower unit is of adequate size and square footage to allow\ntransient rental use.\n\n  Section 4.  Pursuant to an opinion by the California Coastal Commission that\nconversion to transient use constitutions a change in intensity in use and should be authorized\n\nPage 12 of 20\n\fpursuant to a Coastal Development permit, and based on all written and oral evidence presented\nto the Planning Commission, the Planning Commission hereby finds that the application has met\nthe required findings for a Coastal Development Permit under Section 9-8.103 of the Code:\n\n(1)  The use is consistent with the goals and objectives of the public access and recreation\npolicies of the California Coastal Act.\n\nThe Coastal Commission opinion letter noted that vacation rentals in the coastal zone is seen\nas providing access to coastal areas and complies with the intent of the California Coastal Act.\n\n(2)  The use is in conformance with the Certified Local Coastal Program for the City of\nAvalon, which includes the Avalon General Plan, the Local Coastal Plan for Avalon, as well as\nthe Avalon Zoning and Affordable Housing Ordinances.\n\nTransient rental use is allowed in any zone with a Transient Rental License, it is supported\n(with appropriate restrictions) by the Coastal Commission and the LCP. The change in use\ncomplies with the Affordable Housing Ordinance because this does not involve an affordable\nhousing project or unit (AMC 9-11.107).\n\n(3)  The use has received all necessary local approvals issuable by the Planning\nCommission, including Growth Ordinance Pre-Approved (in concept) together with water\nallocation, as may be required.\n\nThe change in use is not considered growth and is not subject to the Growth Ordinance and SCE\nhas determined that the change in use does not require an additional water allocation. The\nCommission had determined that limiting occupancy to two (2) per bedroom plus additional\ntwo (2) occupancy during any stage of fresh water restrictions is appropriate. The only local\napproval issuable by the Planning Commission that is applicable to the project is the requested\nCoastal Development Permit and Transient Rental License. There is no saturation analysis\nconcern, as less than a majority of properties adjacent to the proposed transient rental on 335\nWrigley Road presently hold a CUP or Transient Rental License, in accordance with AMC § 3-\n1.310(e)(5).\n\nThe application is allowed under the 2023 Growth Policy.\n\n(4)  The use has been cleared with respect to environmental regulations of CEQA, as\nnecessary.\n\nThe proposed use is categorically exempt under CEQA Section 15305 of the State CEQA\nGuidelines – Minor Alteration in Land Use Limitations (i.e., CUP for transient rental) which\ndoes not result in change in land use and density.\n\nPage 13 of 20\n\f(5)  For all development seaward of the nearest public highway to the shoreline, the\nproposed development conforms to the public access and recreation policies of Chapter 3 of\nthe Coastal Act.\n\nThe project is not located seaward of the nearest public highway to the shoreline, and is\nconsistent with Chapter 3 of the Coastal Act, as stated in the opinion letter referenced above\nbecause the rental use promotes public access to the coast.\n\n  Section 5.  Based on the entire record before the Planning Commission and all written\nand oral evidence presented to the Planning Commission, the Planning Commission hereby finds\nthe nature and extent of the dedications, reservations, impact fees any other exactions set in\nExhibit “A” are reasonable related to the public needs and roughly proportional to the impacts\ncreated by use of the site.\n\n  Section 6.  Based on the entire record before the Planning Commission and all written\nand oral evidence presented to the Planning Commission, the Planning Commission hereby finds\nthat the Project is categorically exempt from CEQA under the following provisions, because the\ntransient rental does not result in changes to land use or density:\n\n  CEQA Guidelines, Section 15305. Minor Alterations to Land Use Limitations.\n\n  Section 7.  Based on the entire record before the Planning Commission and all written\nand oral evidence presented to the Planning Commission, the Planning Commission hereby finds\nthe property Site is within the 300-foot appealable zone which allows for appeal of the Coastal\nDevelopment Permit to the Coastal Commission.\n\n  Section 8.  Pursuant to Section 3-1.311 of the Code, the Transient Rental License shall\nbecome effective fifteen (15) days after the receipt by the Applicant of a written notice of action.\n\n  Section 9.  The Chairman of the Planning Commission shall sign this Resolution 23-01\nand the Secretary of the Planning Commission shall certify the adoption thereof.\n\n  BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED that a copy of this Resolution 23-01 be delivered to the City\nClerk of Avalon and a copy be sent to the Applicant at the address set forth in the application for\nthe Transient Rental License.\n\nPASSED, APPROVED AND ADOPTED this 18th day of, January, 2023.\n\n  ________________________\n  Chairman Bart Glass\n\nATTEST:\n__________________________\n\nDavid Maistros, City Manager\nPage 14 of 20\n\f  I, Donna Terpinitz-Romo, hereby certify that the foregoing Resolution 23-01 was adopted\nby the Planning Commission of the City of Avalon at a regular meeting held on the\nJanuary 18, 2023 by the following vote, to wit:\n\nAYES:\nNOES:\nABSENT:\nABSTAIN:\n\n  _____________________________\n  Donna Terpinitz-Romo, Planning Administrative Assistant\n\nPage 15 of 20\n\f  EXHIBIT “A”\n\n  STANDARD LIST OF CONDITIONS\n  FOR TRANSIENT RENTAL LICENSE\n\n  Street Address: 335 Wrigley Road\n\nPursuant to AMC Sec. 3-1.317 – Requirements and Standards Applicable to All Transient\nRentals, the following apply:\n\na) Registration, Inspection, and Licensing Requirements.\n  1. Any managing agency, agent, owner or property manager who rents one (1) or more\n  transient rentals shall be required to obtain a City business license. Failure to obtain a\n  business license or continuing to operate a transient rental after revocation of a\n  business license constitutes an infraction pursuant to Section 1- 2. 01(b) of the\n  Municipal Code, and may result in imposition of a fine upon conviction among other\n  penalties.\n  2. All transient rentals shall be subject to collection of transient occupancy tax as required\n  by the Municipal Code, and each managing agency, agent, owner and property manager\n  of a transient rental shall collect and make payment to the City of the transient\n  occupancy tax for their transient rental.\n\nb) Sign Requirements. Each transient rental unit shall have posted in a location that is clearly\n  visible from the outside of the property one (1) sign with the contents set forth below. The\n  sign shall either be purchased at a reasonable cost through the City of Avalon Planning\n  Department or consistent with a template provided by the City. The sign shall be made of\n  material which affixes to a window, not to exceed two (2) square feet in size, have lettering\n  of sufficient size to be easily read from a place accessible to the public, and shall clearly\n  contain the following information:\n  1. Street address of the transient rental unit;\n  2. Allowed maximum occupancy for the transient rental unit; and\n  3. Name of the managing agency, agent, property manager, or owner of the unit, and a\n  local telephone number at which that party or his agent may be reached on a 24-hour\n  basis.\n\nc) Advertising Requirements. Any sign advertising the available use of a transient rental unit\n  must meet all of the advertising and sign requirements found in the Avalon Municipal Code.\n\nd) Notice and Rental Agreement Requirements.\n  1. Each transient rental unit shall have a clearly visible and legible notice posted within the\n  unit on or adjacent to the front door, containing the following information:\nPage 16 of 20\n\f  a. The name of the managing agency, agent, property manager, or owner of the\n  unit, and a local telephone number at which that party or his agent may be\n  reached on a 24-hour basis;\n  b. The maximum number of occupants permitted to stay in the unit;\n  c. Notification that trash and refuse shall not be left or stored on the property, the\n  scheduled dates and times for pick-up at the location, and an explanation of the\n  City's recycling program;\n  d. Notification that failure to conform to the parking and occupancy requirements\n  of the structure is a violation of this section;\n  e. When the City has enacted water conservancy measures by ordinance, notice of\n  the requirements of such ordinance and a request that occupants conserve\n  water as much as possible in the use of showers, baths, faucets and laundry;\n  f. Notification that use of unenclosed decks and patios between 10: 00 p.m. and\n  8:00 a.m. is prohibited where such use generates noise which would disturb or\n  annoy a reasonable person occupying the adjacent properties; and\n  g. A statement that Avalon has a \"zero tolerance\" policy towards nuisances, rowdy\n  and disruptive behavior and advising that the occupancy will be immediately\n  terminated for violations of this policy.\n  2. The foregoing information shall be stated in the rental information and agreement\n  provided to prospective renters prior to their occupancy of the unit.\n\ne) Vehicles. Off-street parking shall be provided on or off site such that all vehicles bearing\n  permits issued for the unit can be parked off street, except when used pursuant to a\n  temporary permit issued to the owner as provided in Title 4. The owner, agent and\n  managing agency shall comply with the Avalon vehicle ordinance which (1) permits\n  transient renters to only use the vehicle which is permitted for the location; and (2)\n  prohibits the owner, agent or managing agency from charging for the use of the vehicle,\n  except:\n  1. Properties located in the \"flats\" (as defined in Section 9-5.406) and properties located in\n  the condominium known as \"Bahia Vista,\" \"Canyon Terrace,\" and \"Sol Vista\" shall not\n  include a vehicle to be used by transient guests.\n\nf) Trash. The owner or managing agent shall provide written information to each transient\n  occupant setting out the schedule for trash pick-up at the location and an explanation of\n  the City's recycling program and ensure that there are adequate materials for disposing of\n  trash in accordance with the City's program. All trash containers shall be secured against\n  animal incursion.\n\ng) Maximum Occupancy. The maximum occupancy for transient rentals shall be two (2)\n  persons (including children and infants) per bedroom, plus two (2); provided the Planning\n  Commission in its discretion may establish a different maximum number where appropriate\n  and reasonable to permit fewer persons that the foregoing formula might otherwise allow\n  and may also increase the number that would otherwise be allowed under the foregoing\n  formula where facts are presented demonstrating that the configuration of the property\n  warrants it. Reasons for establishing a different maximum occupancy number include, but\n\nPage 17 of 20\n\f  are not limited to:\n  1. Size and location of the property;\n  2. Distinction between single- and multi-unit properties; and\n  3. Number and size of each sleeping room in the transient rental unit.\n\nh) Unenclosed areas. The use of unenclosed areas, such as decks and balconies, attached to\n  each transient rental unit shall be prohibited between the hours of 10:00 p.m. and 8:00 a.m.\n  Monday through Sunday.\n\ni) Code Compliance, Fire Safety and Inspection. Each dwelling unit used for transient rental\n  within the City is subject to all of the following inspection requirements:\n  1. The owner shall be responsible for compliance with all codes regarding Fire, Building\n  and Safety, Health and Safety and other relevant laws and the requirements of\n  paragraph (8) of this subsection.\n  2. Any transient rental unit which was permitted or legally nonconforming and in existence\n  prior to the effective date of this subsection shall be made available for inspection\n  within thirty (30) days after the effective date of this subsection. The City shall endeavor\n  to inspect all such units within one hundred eighty (180) days of the effective date of\n  this subsection. The Planning Department shall coordinate with the property owner and\n  the inspectors to arrange for notice and the date of inspection. Within fifteen (15) days\n  following inspection, the inspectors shall submit an original, signed inspection report to\n  the Planning Department, or, where applicable, provide notice that the property owner\n  has unreasonably prevented inspection. Failure to cooperate with the scheduling of the\n  inspection shall subject the approval to use the unit as a transient rental to revocation.\n  Any new transient rental unit established after the effective date of this subsection shall\n  be inspected prior to commencement of the use, which inspection shall be scheduled\n  and conducted within thirty (30) days of approval. In addition to the registration for\n  each unit as required in subsection (a) of this section, an original, signed inspection\n  report for each unit shall be submitted to the Director of Finance in order to obtain\n  approval to use the unit as a transient rental.\n  3. Inspections shall be conducted by a qualified inspector(s) authorized and approved by\n  the City to conduct such inspections. Inspectors shall demonstrate appropriate\n  qualifications to conduct the required inspections as determined by the Building Official\n  or the Fire Department, as applicable. The cost of such inspections, plus any\n  administrative charges shall be paid by the owner pursuant to the City's adopted fee\n  schedule. The City shall make every reasonable effort to ensure that inspections are\n  completed in a timely manner.\n  4. After the initial inspection, each rental unit shall be re-inspected no less than once every\n  three (3) years following the original inspection. Verification of inspection will be done\n  at the time of business license renewal. Failure to submit inspection reports shall result\n  in revocation of approval to use the unit for transient rentals.\n  5. Inspections shall verify compliance with all applicable provisions of this section.\n  6. If upon completing an inspection of a transient rental, the inspector(s) determines that\n  the unit is not in conformance with this section, the inspector shall provide the\n  managing agency, agent, owner or property manager with a list of required corrections,\n\nPage 18 of 20\n\f  set a reasonable time for correction and a date for re-inspection. A copy of the\n  foregoing shall also be given to the Planning Department. If the unit is registered with\n  the City as a transient rental, approval by the City to use the unit for transient rental\n  shall be suspended until correction and re-inspection if determined by the inspector to\n  present an immediate health and safety hazard, and submission of a new inspection\n  report for the unit indicating compliance with this section. If the unit is not yet\n  registered with or approved by the City, approval for its use as a transient rental shall be\n  withheld until submission of a new inspection report for the unit indicating compliance\n  with this section.\n  7. Noncompliance by an owner, managing agency, property manager or agent of the\n  requirements for registration, inspection and operation of transient rentals may result\n  in the revocation of the business license and Transient Rental License authorizing the\n  use, and upon revocation, no new business license or Transient Rental License may be\n  issued to the same transient rental licensee for a period of twelve (12) months following\n  such revocation.\n  8. The following requirements for transient rentals will be evaluated upon inspection of\n  each unit, and shall constitute minimum requirements. The unit must be brought into\n  conformance with these requirements and any other applicable codes and ordinances in\n  order for the use to be allowed.\n  a. Required sign and notice must be posted, and address of unit must be legible from\n  outside the property unless exempted.\n  b. Smoke detectors shall be installed within each sleeping room and at a point centrally\n  located in the corridor or area giving access to each separate sleeping room. Battery-\n  operated smoke detectors are acceptable, provided that they are maintained in\n  good working order at all times, or except as required by other applicable codes.\n  Where fire suppression systems have been installed, such systems shall also be\n  subject to the inspection and testing requirements set forth in Title 4, Chapter 3.\n  c. The transient rental shall be equipped with a minimum of one (1) 2A. 1 OB:C type\n  extinguisher with seventy- five ( 75') feet of travel distance to all portions of the\n  structure; there shall be no less than one ( 1) such extinguisher per floor. Fire\n  extinguishers shall be mounted in visible locations with the tops of the extinguishers\n  mounted between three (3') feet and five (5') feet above the floor and shall be\n  accessible to occupants at all times. California State Fire Marshal annual certification\n  tags must be provided and be current on all extinguishers.\n  d. Any locking mechanism on outside doors must be operable from inside the unit\n  without the use of a key. If the dwelling unit is greater than three thousand (3,000)\n  square feet in area, two (2) exit doors shall be required, each of which shall conform\n  to this requirement.\n  e. Transient rentals shall be maintained in a clean and sanitary condition and free from\n  hazards, which shall include meeting the following requirements:\n  i. There shall be no exposed wiring, improper wiring materials, or overloaded\n  electrical circuits;\n  ii. There shall be no permanent use of extension cords for appliances, heaters,\n  lamps or other fixtures;\n  iii. There shall be no leaking fixtures, clogged or leaking wastewater lines;\n\nPage 19 of 20\n\f  iv.  Faucets and fixtures shall be maintained in working conditions;\n  v.  Showers, sinks and bathing facilities shall be clean and properly maintained;\n  vi.  There shall be no evidence of pest infestations;\n  vii.  If directed by the Fire Department, bedroom windows shall be operable to allow\n  for emergency egress;\n  viii.  There shall be at least one (I) window per bedroom, to allow for proper interior\n  ventilation;\n  ix.  There shall be no accumulation of trash and/or debris on the site or within the\n  unit;\n  x.  All steps, stairways, decks and railings shall be stable and structurally sound;\n  xi.  All standard appliances, including but not limited to kitchen appliances, furnaces\n  and water heaters, shall be operational;\n  xii.  Any hot tubs, pools and spas shall be fenced or equipped with an approved cover\n  with locking mechanisms as required by State law, and shall be maintained in a\n  safe and sanitary condition;\n  xiii.  Any lofts or attic conversions shall be provided with acceptable exiting and head\n  clearance; stairs or providing access to these areas shall be safe and structurally\n  sound; and\n  xiv.  Exits shall remain clear of storage, debris or impedance at all times.\n\n  9. The managing agency, agent, property manager, or owner of the unit whose name\n  and local telephone number appears on the sign pursuant to subsection (b) of this\n  section must maintain a physical presence within the geographical limits of the City of\n  Avalon during the term of the transient rental unit lease in order to provide prompt\n  response to complaints.\n\n  10. If applicable, confirmation is provided by SCE that this project will not require\n  additional fresh water.\n\n  11. Indemnification of City\n  a. The owner shall defend, indemnify and hold the City and its employees and agents\n  harmless from any action, lawsuit or proceeding challenging the City’s action on\n  the Transient Rental License, Coastal Development Permit, and/or CEQA finding.\n\nPage 20 of 20","minutes_text":"PK\u0003\u0004\u0014\u0000\u0006\u0000\b\u0000\u0000\u0000!\u00002�oWf\u0001\u0000\u0000�\u000